        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0080', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=200 and Mode=215. What relation between Mean and Mode is consistent with the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient?', 'a) Mean < Mode; negative skewness', 'b) Mean > Mode; positive skewness', 'c) Mean = Mode; zero skewness', 'd) Mean < Mode; positive skewness', 'a', 1, 'Use Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D.; S.D. is positive, so inspect Mean−Mode.', 'Because S.D. is positive, the sign of Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is determined by Mean−Mode. Here Mean−Mode=-15, so Mean < Mode and the coefficient is negative. The option B reverses the Mean–Mode relation and sign. The option C incorrectly makes the unequal values equal. The option D keeps the direction of the inequality but assigns the wrong skewness sign.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0081', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=24 and the median is Q₂=31. Find Q₃.', 'a) 38', 'b) 17', 'c) 55', 'd) 31', 'a', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(31)−24=38. The option B uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0082', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=38 and the median is Q₂=46. Find Q₃.', 'a) 30', 'b) 54', 'c) 84', 'd) 46', 'b', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(46)−38=54. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0083', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=52 and the median is Q₂=61. Find Q₃.', 'a) 43', 'b) 113', 'c) 70', 'd) 61', 'c', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(61)−52=70. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option B uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0084', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=64 and the median is Q₂=73. Find Q₃.', 'a) 55', 'b) 137', 'c) 73', 'd) 82', 'd', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(73)−64=82. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option B uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option C stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0085', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=16 and the median is Q₂=25. Find Q₃.', 'a) 34', 'b) 7', 'c) 41', 'd) 25', 'a', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(25)−16=34. The option B uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0086', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=70 and the median is Q₂=82. Find Q₃.', 'a) 58', 'b) 94', 'c) 152', 'd) 82', 'b', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(82)−70=94. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0087', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=83 and the median is Q₂=95. Find Q₃.', 'a) 71', 'b) 178', 'c) 107', 'd) 95', 'c', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(95)−83=107. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option B uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0088', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=47 and the median is Q₂=59. Find Q₃.', 'a) 35', 'b) 106', 'c) 59', 'd) 71', 'd', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(59)−47=71. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option B uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option C stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0089', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=92 and the median is Q₂=104. Find Q₃.', 'a) 116', 'b) 80', 'c) 196', 'd) 104', 'a', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(104)−92=116. The option B uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0090', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, the lower quartile is Q₁=110 and the median is Q₂=123. Find Q₃.', 'a) 97', 'b) 136', 'c) 233', 'd) 123', 'b', 1, 'For symmetry set Skᵦ=0 and rearrange Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0.', 'Symmetry gives Skᵦ=0, so Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0. Rearranging, Q₃=2Q₂−Q₁=2(123)−110=136. The option A uses 2Q₁−Q₂. The option C uses Q₁+Q₂ instead of the required relation. The option D stops at Q₂ instead of finding Q₃.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0091', 3, 'If Q₁=8, Q₂=13 and Q₃=18, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 0', 'c) 2.6', 'd) -1', 'b', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 5. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(18−8)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option C comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0092', 3, 'If Q₁=14, Q₂=22 and Q₃=30, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 2.75', 'c) 0', 'd) -1', 'c', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 8. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(30−14)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option B comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0093', 3, 'If Q₁=21, Q₂=30 and Q₃=39, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 3.3333', 'c) -1', 'd) 0', 'd', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 9. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(39−21)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option B comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option C comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0094', 3, 'If Q₁=27, Q₂=37 and Q₃=47, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 0', 'b) 1', 'c) 3.7', 'd) -1', 'a', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 10. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(47−27)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option B comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option C comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0095', 3, 'If Q₁=33, Q₂=44 and Q₃=55, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 0', 'c) 4', 'd) -1', 'b', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 11. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(55−33)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option C comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0096', 3, 'If Q₁=41, Q₂=53 and Q₃=65, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 4.4167', 'c) 0', 'd) -1', 'c', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 12. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(65−41)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option B comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0097', 3, 'If Q₁=48, Q₂=61 and Q₃=74, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 4.6923', 'c) -1', 'd) 0', 'd', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 13. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(74−48)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option B comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option C comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0098', 3, 'If Q₁=56, Q₂=70 and Q₃=84, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 0', 'b) 1', 'c) 5', 'd) -1', 'a', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 14. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(84−56)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option B comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option C comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0099', 3, 'If Q₁=63, Q₂=78 and Q₃=93, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 0', 'c) 5.2', 'd) -1', 'b', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 15. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(93−63)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option C comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0100', 3, 'If Q₁=72, Q₂=88 and Q₃=104, the three quartiles are in arithmetic progression. Calculate Bowley’s coefficient and state the nature of the distribution.', 'a) 1', 'b) 5.5', 'c) 0', 'd) -1', 'c', 2, 'For quartiles in A.P., compare Q₃−Q₂ and Q₂−Q₁, then substitute in Bowley’s coefficient.', 'Because Q₁, Q₂ and Q₃ are in A.P., Q₃−Q₂=Q₂−Q₁. Here both differences equal 16. Hence Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂=0 and Skᵦ=0/(104−72)=0, so the distribution is symmetric. The option A comes from using the ratio of the two quartile gaps instead of Bowley’s formula. The option B comes from omitting the −2Q₂ term. The option D comes from reversing the sign of the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0101', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=55 and Mode=48. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'c', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=7>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0102', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=68 and Mode=61. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=7>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option C gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0103', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=82 and Mode=74. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'b) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'c) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'a', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=8>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option B reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option C incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0104', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=96 and Mode=89. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'c) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'b', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=7>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option C incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0105', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=115 and Mode=107. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'c', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=8>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0106', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=132 and Mode=125. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=7>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option C gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0107', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=149 and Mode=138. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'b) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'c) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'a', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=11>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option B reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option C incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0108', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=170 and Mode=158. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'c) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'b', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=12>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option C incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0109', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=205 and Mode=194. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'c', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=11>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option D gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0110', 3, 'A distribution has Mean=230 and Mode=218. Determine the sign of Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness and the nature of the distribution.', 'a) Skₚ<0; negatively skewed', 'b) Skₚ=0; symmetric', 'c) Skₚ>0; negatively skewed', 'd) Skₚ>0; positively skewed', 'd', 1, 'Use the sign of Mean−Mode; S.D. is always positive.', 'Mean−Mode=12>0. Since S.D. is positive, Skₚ=(Mean−Mode)/S.D. is also positive. Therefore the distribution is positively skewed. The option A reverses the sign of Mean−Mode. The option B incorrectly treats unequal mean and mode as equal. The option C gets the coefficient sign right but assigns the wrong nature.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0111', 3, 'Skewness is a numerical measure that indicates which feature of a distribution?', 'a) It measures only dispersion', 'b) It measures only the arithmetic mean', 'c) It indicates symmetry/asymmetry and, if asymmetric, its extent and direction', 'd) It measures only the total frequency', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: It indicates symmetry/asymmetry and, if asymmetric, its extent and direction. Option A is incorrect because it states It measures only dispersion, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states It measures only the arithmetic mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states It measures only the total frequency, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0112', 3, 'A distribution is symmetric when values equidistant from the mean have what frequency pattern?', 'a) Zero frequency for every value below the mean', 'b) Higher frequencies only above the mean', 'c) Higher frequencies only below the mean', 'd) Equal frequencies for values equidistant from the mean', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: Equal frequencies for values equidistant from the mean. Option A is incorrect because it states Zero frequency for every value below the mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Higher frequencies only above the mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Higher frequencies only below the mean, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0113', 3, 'For a symmetric distribution, which relation among the three measures of central tendency holds?', 'a) Mean = Median = Mode', 'b) Mean > Median > Mode', 'c) Mode > Median > Mean', 'd) Mean = Mode > Median', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: Mean = Median = Mode. Option B is incorrect because it states Mean > Median > Mode, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Mode > Median > Mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Mean = Mode > Median, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0114', 3, 'A distribution is positively skewed when which frequency pattern occurs relative to the mean?', 'a) Values above the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values below the mean', 'b) Values below the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values above the mean', 'c) All frequencies are equal', 'd) Only the mean is greater than the mode', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: Values below the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values above the mean. Option A is incorrect because it states Values above the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values below the mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states All frequencies are equal, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Only the mean is greater than the mode, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0115', 3, 'In a positively skewed distribution, which tail is longer?', 'a) Left tail', 'b) Both tails must be equal', 'c) Right tail', 'd) No tail', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: Right tail. Option A is incorrect because it states Left tail, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Both tails must be equal, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states No tail, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0116', 3, 'Which order of mean, median and mode corresponds to positive skewness?', 'a) Mode > Median > Mean', 'b) Mean = Median = Mode', 'c) Median > Mean > Mode', 'd) Mean > Median > Mode', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: Mean > Median > Mode. Option A is incorrect because it states Mode > Median > Mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Mean = Median = Mode, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Median > Mean > Mode, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0117', 3, 'A distribution is negatively skewed when which frequency pattern occurs relative to the mean?', 'a) Values above the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values below the mean', 'b) Values below the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values above the mean', 'c) All equidistant values have equal frequencies', 'd) The right tail is necessarily longer', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: Values above the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values below the mean. Option B is incorrect because it states Values below the mean have higher frequencies than corresponding values above the mean, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states All equidistant values have equal frequencies, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states The right tail is necessarily longer, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0118', 3, 'In a negatively skewed distribution, which tail is longer?', 'a) Right tail', 'b) Left tail', 'c) Both tails must be equal', 'd) Neither tail', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: Left tail. Option A is incorrect because it states Right tail, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Both tails must be equal, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Neither tail, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0119', 3, 'Which order of mode, median and mean corresponds to negative skewness?', 'a) Mean > Median > Mode', 'b) Mean = Median = Mode', 'c) Mode > Median > Mean', 'd) Median > Mode > Mean', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: Mode > Median > Mean. Option A is incorrect because it states Mean > Median > Mode, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Mean = Median = Mode, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Median > Mode > Mean, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0120', 3, 'Which formula defines Karl Pearson’s coefficient of skewness when mode is available?', 'a) (Mean+Mode)/S.D.', 'b) (Mean−Mode)/Variance', 'c) (Mode−Mean)×S.D.', 'd) (Mean−Mode)/S.D.', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: (Mean−Mode)/S.D.. Option A is incorrect because it states (Mean+Mode)/S.D., which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states (Mean−Mode)/Variance, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states (Mode−Mean)×S.D., which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0121', 3, 'Why is the difference Mean−Mode divided by S.D. in Pearson’s coefficient?', 'a) To make comparison possible across distributions of different nature', 'b) To increase the units of the coefficient', 'c) To make the coefficient equal to the variance', 'd) To remove the median from every calculation', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: To make comparison possible across distributions of different nature. Option B is incorrect because it states To increase the units of the coefficient, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states To make the coefficient equal to the variance, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states To remove the median from every calculation, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0122', 3, 'Which statement about the units of Pearson’s coefficient is correct?', 'a) It always has units of the original variable', 'b) It is free from the units of the data values', 'c) It always has square units', 'd) Its units equal the variance units', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: It is free from the units of the data values. Option A is incorrect because it states It always has units of the original variable, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states It always has square units, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Its units equal the variance units, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0123', 3, 'What is the Pearson coefficient for a symmetric distribution?', 'a) Skₚ>0', 'b) Skₚ<0', 'c) Skₚ=0', 'd) Skₚ=1', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: Skₚ=0. Option A is incorrect because it states Skₚ>0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Skₚ<0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Skₚ=1, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0124', 3, 'What inequality indicates positive skewness for Pearson’s coefficient?', 'a) Skₚ<0', 'b) Skₚ=0', 'c) Skₚ<−1', 'd) Skₚ>0', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: Skₚ>0. Option A is incorrect because it states Skₚ<0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Skₚ=0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Skₚ<−1, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0125', 3, 'What inequality indicates negative skewness for Pearson’s coefficient?', 'a) Skₚ<0', 'b) Skₚ>0', 'c) Skₚ=0', 'd) Skₚ>1', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: Skₚ<0. Option B is incorrect because it states Skₚ>0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Skₚ=0, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Skₚ>1, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0126', 3, 'Which range statement about Pearson’s coefficient matches the stated properties?', 'a) All values lie between −1 and 1', 'b) Most values lie between −1 and 1, while all values lie between −3 and 3', 'c) All values lie between −3 and 3 and most are outside −1 to 1', 'd) The coefficient is always non-negative', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: Most values lie between −1 and 1, while all values lie between −3 and 3. Option A is incorrect because it states All values lie between −1 and 1, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states All values lie between −3 and 3 and most are outside −1 to 1, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states The coefficient is always non-negative, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0127', 3, 'When mode is indeterminate, which relation is used to obtain Pearson’s coefficient from mean and median?', 'a) Mean−Mode = Mean−Median, so Skₚ=(Mean−Median)/S.D.', 'b) Mean−Mode = 2(Mean−Median), so Skₚ=2(Mean−Median)/S.D.', 'c) Mean−Mode = 3(Mean−Median), so Skₚ=3(Mean−Median)/S.D.', 'd) Mean−Mode = 3(Mode−Median), so Skₚ=3(Mode−Median)/S.D.', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: Mean−Mode = 3(Mean−Median), so Skₚ=3(Mean−Median)/S.D.. Option A is incorrect because it states Mean−Mode = Mean−Median, so Skₚ=(Mean−Median)/S.D., which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states Mean−Mode = 2(Mean−Median), so Skₚ=2(Mean−Median)/S.D., which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Mean−Mode = 3(Mode−Median), so Skₚ=3(Mode−Median)/S.D., which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0128', 3, 'Which formula defines Bowley’s coefficient of skewness?', 'a) (Q₃−Q₁)/(Q₃+Q₁)', 'b) (Q₃+Q₂−2Q₁)/(Q₃−Q₁)', 'c) (Q₃−Q₂)/(Q₂−Q₁)', 'd) (Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂)/(Q₃−Q₁)', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: (Q₃+Q₁−2Q₂)/(Q₃−Q₁). Option A is incorrect because it states (Q₃−Q₁)/(Q₃+Q₁), which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states (Q₃+Q₂−2Q₁)/(Q₃−Q₁), which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states (Q₃−Q₂)/(Q₂−Q₁), which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0129', 3, 'For positive skewness, how do the two quartile gaps compare?', 'a) Q₃−Q₂ > Q₂−Q₁', 'b) Q₃−Q₂ < Q₂−Q₁', 'c) Q₃−Q₂ = Q₂−Q₁', 'd) Q₃−Q₁ < 0', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: Q₃−Q₂ > Q₂−Q₁. Option B is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₂ < Q₂−Q₁, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₂ = Q₂−Q₁, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₁ < 0, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0130', 3, 'For negative skewness, how do the two quartile gaps compare?', 'a) Q₃−Q₂ > Q₂−Q₁', 'b) Q₃−Q₂ < Q₂−Q₁', 'c) Q₃−Q₂ = Q₂−Q₁', 'd) Q₃−Q₁ < 0', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: Q₃−Q₂ < Q₂−Q₁. Option A is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₂ > Q₂−Q₁, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₂ = Q₂−Q₁, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Q₃−Q₁ < 0, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0131', 3, 'What does Skᵦ=0 indicate?', 'a) A positively skewed distribution', 'b) A negatively skewed distribution', 'c) A symmetric distribution', 'd) An indeterminate distribution', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, C: A symmetric distribution. Option A is incorrect because it states A positively skewed distribution, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states A negatively skewed distribution, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states An indeterminate distribution, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0132', 3, 'What range is stated for Bowley’s coefficient?', 'a) −3 < Skᵦ < 3', 'b) 0 < Skᵦ < 1', 'c) Skᵦ≤−1 or Skᵦ≥1', 'd) −1 < Skᵦ < 1', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, D: −1 < Skᵦ < 1. Option A is incorrect because it states −3 < Skᵦ < 3, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option B is incorrect because it states 0 < Skᵦ < 1, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Skᵦ≤−1 or Skᵦ≥1, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0133', 3, 'Why can Bowley’s coefficient be computed when open-ended classes occur at the extremities?', 'a) Open-ended classes at the extremities do not affect the quartiles used in Skᵦ', 'b) Open-ended classes make quartiles equal', 'c) Bowley’s formula does not use quartiles', 'd) Open-ended classes force Skᵦ to zero', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, A: Open-ended classes at the extremities do not affect the quartiles used in Skᵦ. Option B is incorrect because it states Open-ended classes make quartiles equal, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states Bowley’s formula does not use quartiles, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states Open-ended classes force Skᵦ to zero, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-03-mcq-0134', 3, 'Which type of distribution is described as often positively skewed because a few observations have very high values?', 'a) Lifespan of individuals', 'b) Salaries of employees', 'c) A perfectly symmetric rod-length distribution', 'd) A distribution with equal frequencies at equidistant values', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated definition or relationship, then compare it with the four alternatives.', 'The stated rule gives the correct choice, B: Salaries of employees. Option A is incorrect because it states Lifespan of individuals, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option C is incorrect because it states A perfectly symmetric rod-length distribution, which conflicts with the required relationship. Option D is incorrect because it states A distribution with equal frequencies at equidistant values, which conflicts with the required relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0001', 4, 'A study records the weekly study hours (X taking values 1, 2, 3) and quiz scores (Y taking values 10, 20, 30) for 28 students. The joint frequencies f(x, y) are given as: f(1, 10)=2, f(1, 20)=3, f(1, 30)=1; f(2, 10)=4, f(2, 20)=5, f(2, 30)=2; f(3, 10)=1, f(3, 20)=6, f(3, 30)=4. What is the marginal frequency of students scoring Y = 20?', '7', '11', '14', '18', 'c', 1, 'Sum the joint frequencies across all values of X for which Y = 20.', 'To find the marginal frequency of Y = 20, sum the frequencies across all values of X for Y = 20: f_y(20) = f(1, 20) + f(2, 20) + f(3, 20) = 3 + 5 + 6 = 14. Option B (11) results from summing the row for X = 2 instead of column Y = 20 (4 + 5 + 2 = 11). Option C (7) results from summing only for Y = 10 (2 + 4 + 1 = 7). Option D (18) results from incorrectly adding the first two values of X=2 (3 + 5 + 6 + 4).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0002', 4, 'In an assessment of 40 employees, experience in years (X taking values 2, 4, 6) and performance rating (Y taking values 1, 2, 3) are recorded. The frequencies for rating Y = 3 are f(2, 3) = 3, f(4, 3) = 7, and f(6, 3) = 5. What is the conditional frequency of employees having 4 years of experience (X = 4) given that their rating is Y = 3?', '15', '4', '12', '7', 'd', 1, 'Identify the specific joint frequency corresponding to X = 4 in the row or sub-distribution where Y = 3.', 'The conditional frequency distribution of X given Y = 3 considers only the subset where Y = 3. The frequency corresponding to X = 4 is directly f(4, 3) = 7. Option A (15) is the total conditional frequency for Y = 3 (3 + 7 + 5). Option C (12) results from summing f(4, 3) + f(6, 3) = 7 + 5. Option D (4) results from confusing the value of the variable X with its frequency.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0003', 4, 'A bivariate frequency distribution of two discrete variables X and Y has cell frequencies: f(1, 10) = 8, f(1, 20) = 12, f(2, 10) = 15, and f(2, 20) = 5. What is the total number of observations (N) in this distribution?', '40', '27', '20', '35', 'a', 1, 'Add the frequencies of all the cells in the bivariate table.', 'The total number of observations N is the sum of all cell frequencies: N = 8 + 12 + 15 + 5 = 40. Option A (20) results from taking only the first row total (8 + 12). Option B (27) results from incorrectly adding the diagonal cells or partial sums (12 + 15). Option D (35) results from omitting f(2, 20) = 5 from the total.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0004', 4, 'The daily cups of coffee consumed (X = 0, 1, 2) and stress levels (Y = 1, 2, 3, 4) of 50 office workers are recorded in a bivariate table. For X = 0, the frequencies across Y = 1, 2, 3, 4 are 3, 4, 1, and 2 respectively. What is the marginal frequency of people who consume 0 cups of coffee (X = 0)?', '7', '10', '8', '12', 'b', 1, 'Sum all cell frequencies associated with X = 0 across all values of Y.', 'The marginal frequency of X = 0 is found by summing the frequencies in the X = 0 column/row: f_x(0) = 3 + 4 + 1 + 2 = 10. Option B (7) results from summing only the first two cells (3 + 4). Option C (8) results from omitting the second cell (3 + 1 + 2 + 2). Option D (12) results from misadding 3 + 4 + 1 + 2 as 12.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0005', 4, 'A traffic department records driver age (X taking values 20, 25, 30) and number of traffic violations (Y taking values 0, 1, 2). For the age group X = 25, the frequencies are f(25, 0) = 14, f(25, 1) = 6, and f(25, 2) = 2. What is the total frequency of violations for drivers aged 25?', '20', '8', '22', '25', 'c', 1, 'Add all frequencies in the conditional distribution where X = 25.', 'The total conditional frequency when X = 25 is the sum of frequencies across all Y values for X = 25: 14 + 6 + 2 = 22. Option A (20) results from adding only 14 + 6 and omitting 2. Option C (8) results from adding only the violation categories 6 + 2. Option D (25) results from confusing the age value 25 with the frequency total.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0006', 4, 'In a reading program for 50 students, books read (X = 1, 2, 3) and performance band (Y = 1, 2, 3) are recorded. For performance band Y = 3, the frequencies are f(1, 3) = 10, f(2, 3) = 6, and f(3, 3) = 4. What percentage of students in band Y = 3 read exactly 1 book?', '20%', '25%', '50%', '10%', 'c', 2, 'Find the total frequency for Y = 3, then divide f(1, 3) by that total and multiply by 100.', 'Total conditional frequency for Y = 3 is 10 + 6 + 4 = 20. The number of students with X = 1 is 10. Thus, the required percentage is (10 / 20) * 100 = 50%. Option A (20%) results from dividing 10 by the grand total 50 (10/50 = 20%). Option C (25%) results from dividing f(3, 3) by the total (4/20 = 20%) or miscalculating 6/24. Option D (10%) results from reading the raw count 10 as a percentage.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0007', 4, 'A sports analyst compiles a bivariate table of goals scored (X = 0, 1, 2) and yellow cards (Y = 0, 1, 2) over 30 matches. The marginal frequency distribution of X is found to be: f(X=0) = 8, f(X=1) = 15, and f(X=2) = 7. What is the difference between the marginal frequency of matches with 1 goal and matches with 2 goals?', '7', '8', '1', '22', 'b', 1, 'Subtract the marginal frequency of X = 2 from the marginal frequency of X = 1.', 'The marginal frequencies are f(X=1) = 15 and f(X=2) = 7. The difference is 15 - 7 = 8. Option A (7) is simply the frequency of X = 2. Option C (1) is the difference between the goal values (2 - 1 = 1) rather than their frequencies. Option D (22) results from adding the frequencies (15 + 7) instead of subtracting.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0008', 4, 'In a bivariate frequency table of discrete variables X (taking values 1, 2, 3) and Y (taking values 1, 2), the marginal total for row Y = 1 is 25. If the cell frequencies are f(1, 1) = 8, f(2, 1) = 11, and f(3, 1) = k, what is the value of k?', '5', '19', '14', '6', 'd', 2, 'The sum of the frequencies in row Y = 1 must equal the marginal total of that row.', 'The marginal total for Y = 1 is f(1, 1) + f(2, 1) + f(3, 1) = 25. Substituting the given values: 8 + 11 + k = 25 ==> 19 + k = 25 ==> k = 6. Option B (19) results from just adding 8 + 11 and stopping. Option C (14) results from subtracting 11 from 25 and ignoring 8. Option D (5) results from an arithmetic subtraction mistake (25 - 20).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0009', 4, 'The following 6 pairs of observations (X, Y) are recorded from an experiment: (2, 5), (2, 8), (4, 5), (2, 5), (4, 8), (2, 5). What is the conditional frequency of Y = 5 when X = 2?', '4', '1', '2', '3', 'd', 2, 'Filter out all observations where X = 2, and count how many times Y = 5 appears among them.', 'The observations where X = 2 are: (2, 5), (2, 8), (2, 5), and (2, 5). Among these 4 observations, Y = 5 appears exactly 3 times. Thus, the conditional frequency is 3. Option A (4) is the total number of observations with X = 2. Option B (1) is the count of observations with X = 2 and Y = 8. Option D (2) results from omitting one occurrence of (2, 5).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0010', 4, 'In a bivariate frequency distribution of employee age (X) and service grade (Y), the marginal frequency for age X = 12 years of service is 16. What is the sum of all frequencies in the conditional frequency distribution of Y given X = 12?', '16', '32', '8', '1', 'a', 2, 'Recall the relationship between the sum of conditional frequencies for a fixed X and the marginal total of that X.', 'By definition, the conditional frequency distribution of Y when X = x_i consists of all cell frequencies in the column/row for that fixed X. The sum of these conditional frequencies is precisely the marginal frequency of that value of X, which is 16. Option A (8) results from incorrectly dividing by 2. Option B (32) results from multiplying by 2. Option D (1) results from confusing frequency with relative frequency/probability.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0011', 4, 'In a grouped bivariate frequency distribution of height in cm (X) and weight in kg (Y), the class interval 150-160 for X has cell frequencies of 7 in class 40-50 for Y, and 9 in class 50-60 for Y. What is the marginal frequency of the class interval 150-160 for height?', '2', '16', '63', '7', 'b', 1, 'Add the frequencies across all weight classes for the height class 150-160.', 'The marginal frequency for class interval 150-160 is obtained by summing its cell frequencies across all Y classes: 7 + 9 = 16. Option B (2) is the difference between the two frequencies (9 - 7). Option C (63) is the product of the frequencies (7 * 9). Option D (7) is only the first cell frequency.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0012', 4, 'A bivariate distribution of weekly study time X (in hours) and exam score Y (out of 100) has the following cell frequencies: for score class 30-50, frequencies are 6 (in 10-20) and 8 (in 20-30); for score class 50-70, frequencies are 10 (in 10-20) and 6 (in 20-30). What is the total frequency for the conditional distribution of X when exam score Y is 50 or more (i.e. Y >= 50)?', '14', '16', '10', '30', 'b', 1, 'Sum the frequencies in the row corresponding to the class interval 50-70.', 'The condition Y >= 50 corresponds to the class interval 50-70. The total frequency for this row is 10 + 6 = 16. Option A (14) is the total frequency for the row 30-50 (6 + 8). Option C (10) is only the frequency of one cell (10-20, 50-70). Option D (30) is the grand total of all observations.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0013', 4, 'Consider the following 5 bivariate observations (X, Y): (12, 42), (18, 55), (14, 48), (25, 45), and (16, 52). If the class intervals are 10-20 and 20-30 for X, and 40-50 and 50-60 for Y, what is the frequency of the cell corresponding to class 10-20 for X and 40-50 for Y?', '1', '3', '2', '4', 'c', 2, 'Count how many pairs have X in [10, 20) and Y in [40, 50).', 'We check each pair: (12, 42) has 10 <= 12 < 20 and 40 <= 42 < 50 (Count = 1). (18, 55) has Y in 50-60. (14, 48) has 10 <= 14 < 20 and 40 <= 48 < 50 (Count = 2). (25, 45) has X = 25 (in 20-30). (16, 52) has Y in 50-60. Thus, exactly 2 observations fall in this cell. Option A (1) misses (14, 48). Option B (3) mistakenly includes (25, 45) or (18, 55). Option D (4) counts all items with X in 10-20.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0014', 4, 'In a grouped bivariate frequency table of monthly income X and expenditure Y, the class interval 100-200 for Y contains frequencies of 5, 12, and 8 across three income classes. What is the marginal frequency of families having expenditure in the class 100-200?', '17', '20', '25', '13', 'c', 1, 'Sum all cell frequencies in the row for expenditure class 100-200.', 'The marginal frequency for class 100-200 of Y is the row sum: 5 + 12 + 8 = 25. Option B (20) results from adding 12 + 8 and omitting 5. Option C (17) results from adding 5 + 12 and omitting 8. Option D (13) results from 5 + 8.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0015', 4, 'The marginal frequency distribution of a grouped variable X is given as: class 10-20 has frequency 15, class 20-30 has frequency 25, and class 30-40 has frequency 10. What is the total number of observations N in the entire bivariate distribution?', '40', '50', '35', '60', 'b', 1, 'The sum of all marginal frequencies of variable X equals the grand total N.', 'The grand total N is the sum of the marginal frequencies of X: N = 15 + 25 + 10 = 50. Option A (40) results from adding only 15 + 25. Option C (35) results from adding 25 + 10. Option D (60) results from an arithmetic error.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0016', 4, 'For students whose height X lies in the interval 145-155 cm, the conditional frequencies of weight Y across classes 35-45 kg, 45-55 kg, and 55-65 kg are 2, 2, and 4 respectively. What proportion of students in this height group have a weight in the class 55-65 kg?', '0.25', '0.50', '0.75', '0.40', 'b', 2, 'Divide the frequency of the class 55-65 by the total conditional frequency for the group 145-155.', 'The total conditional frequency for height class 145-155 is 2 + 2 + 4 = 8. The frequency for weight class 55-65 is 4. The proportion is 4 / 8 = 0.50. Option A (0.25) results from taking 2 / 8. Option B (0.75) results from (2 + 4) / 8. Option D (0.40) results from dividing 4 by 10.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0017', 4, 'A researcher classifies continuous variable X into 4 class intervals and continuous variable Y into 3 class intervals. How many cells will the bivariate frequency distribution table contain (excluding row and column total cells)?', '7', '9', '14', '12', 'd', 1, 'Multiply the number of class intervals of X by the number of class intervals of Y.', 'If variable X has m classes and variable Y has n classes, the bivariate frequency table contains m * n cells. Here, m = 4 and n = 3, so there are 4 * 3 = 12 cells. Option A (7) results from adding m + n (4 + 3). Option C (14) results from 2*(m + n). Option D (9) results from squaring n (3^2).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0018', 4, 'In a grouped bivariate frequency table, the marginal total for class 20-30 of variable X is 18. The column contains three classes for Y with frequencies 4, f, and 5 respectively. What is the value of f?', '11', '8', '9', '7', 'c', 1, 'The sum of all cell frequencies in a column equals the marginal column total.', 'The sum of frequencies in the column for X (20-30) is 4 + f + 5 = 18. This gives 9 + f = 18 ==> f = 9. Option B (8) is an off-by-one arithmetic error. Option C (11) results from subtracting 7 from 18. Option D (7) results from subtracting 11 from 18.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0019', 4, 'The conditional frequency distribution of height X for individuals with weight Y >= 55 kg has frequencies 4, 4, and 4 in classes 135-145, 145-155, and 155-165 cm respectively. How many individuals in this group have a height of less than 155 cm?', '4', '8', '12', '6', 'b', 2, 'Add the frequencies of the classes that lie below 155 cm (i.e. 135-145 and 145-155).', 'Height less than 155 cm comprises the two classes: 135-145 and 145-155. Their frequencies under Y >= 55 kg are 4 and 4. Sum = 4 + 4 = 8. Option A (4) considers only one class (135-145). Option B (12) is the total across all three classes (4 + 4 + 4). Option D (6) is an arbitrary guess.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0020', 4, 'A conditional distribution of variable Y for a given class of X has the following grouped frequencies: 10-20 (frequency 3), 20-30 (frequency 5), 30-40 (frequency 8), and 40-50 (frequency 4). In which class interval does the 10th observation (the median position) lie?', '20-30', '30-40', '10-20', '40-50', 'b', 2, 'Find the cumulative frequencies of the classes in order until the cumulative total reaches or exceeds 10.', 'We calculate cumulative frequencies: Class 10-20: 3; Class 20-30: 3 + 5 = 8; Class 30-40: 8 + 8 = 16. Since the 10th observation falls between cumulative counts 9 and 16, it lies in the class interval 30-40. Option A (20-30) only reaches up to the 8th observation. Option C (10-20) only covers up to the 3rd observation. Option D (40-50) covers observations 17 through 20.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0021', 4, 'A survey of 200 consumers categorizes respondents by gender (90 Men, 110 Women) and brand preference (Brand X, Brand Y). If 35 Men prefer Brand X, how many Men prefer Brand Y?', '75', '55', '45', '65', 'b', 1, 'Subtract the number of Men who prefer Brand X from the total number of Men.', 'The row total for Men is 90. Since 35 Men prefer Brand X, the number of Men preferring Brand Y is 90 - 35 = 55. Option A (75) results from subtracting 35 from Women''s total (110 - 35). Option C (45) results from 80 - 35. Option D (65) results from subtracting 35 from 100.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0022', 4, 'A survey of 150 moviegoers classifies them as Children (60) or Adults (90), and records snack choice (Popcorn or Nachos). If 50 Adults prefer Popcorn, how many Adults prefer Nachos?', '40', '10', '35', '60', 'a', 1, 'Subtract the adults who prefer Popcorn from the total number of adults.', 'The total number of Adults is 90. The number of Adults who prefer Popcorn is 50. Therefore, the number of Adults preferring Nachos is 90 - 50 = 40. Option B (10) results from subtracting 50 from 60 (children total). Option C (35) results from arbitrary miscalculation. Option D (60) is the total number of children.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0023', 4, 'In a market survey of 180 respondents, 40 teenagers and 50 adults stated that they prefer chocolate flavor over vanilla. What is the total column frequency of respondents who prefer chocolate flavor?', '72', '90', '108', '100', 'b', 1, 'Add the number of teenagers preferring chocolate and adults preferring chocolate.', 'The column total for chocolate preference is obtained by summing across age groups: 40 + 50 = 90. Option A (72) is the total number of teenagers. Option B (108) is the total number of adults. Option D (100) results from misadding 40 + 50 as 100.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0024', 4, 'A school surveys 300 students about their primary sport (Football or Cricket). There are 160 High Schoolers and 140 Middle Schoolers. Overall, 120 students play Football and 180 play Cricket. If 70 High Schoolers play Football, how many Middle Schoolers play Cricket?', '50', '70', '90', '110', 'c', 2, 'First find how many middle schoolers play football, then subtract that from the total number of middle schoolers.', 'Total football players = 120. Since 70 high schoolers play football, middle schoolers playing football = 120 - 70 = 50. The total number of middle schoolers is 140, so middle schoolers playing cricket = 140 - 50 = 90. Option A (50) is the number of middle schoolers playing football. Option B (70) is high schoolers playing football. Option C (110) is high schoolers playing cricket (180 - 70 or 160 - 50 = 110).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0025', 4, 'In a 2x2 contingency table classifying 250 individuals by age group (Teenager, Adult) and beverage preference (Tea, Coffee), exactly 105 individuals are Teenagers. What is the marginal row total for Adults?', '150', '155', '135', '145', 'd', 1, 'Subtract the total number of teenagers from the grand total N.', 'The sum of the row totals must equal the grand total: Teenagers + Adults = 250 ==> 105 + Adults = 250 ==> Adults = 145. Option B (155) is an arithmetic error (borrowing mistake). Option C (135) results from 240 - 105. Option D (150) results from 255 - 105.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0026', 4, 'Out of 400 surveyed college students, 220 are Boys and 180 are Girls. Each student chooses either Music or Sports. If 130 Boys chose Sports and 80 Girls chose Music, what is the total number of students who chose Music?', '150', '190', '210', '170', 'd', 2, 'First determine how many boys chose Music by subtracting boys choosing Sports from total boys, then add girls choosing Music.', 'Number of Boys choosing Music = Total Boys - Boys choosing Sports = 220 - 130 = 90. Girls choosing Music = 80. Total students choosing Music = 90 + 80 = 170. Option B (190) results from adding 130 + 60. Option C (210) results from 400 - 190. Option D (150) results from 220 - 70.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0027', 4, 'In a clinical trial with 500 patients, 60% received Drug A and the rest received Drug B. Among the patients who received Drug A, 75% showed improvement while the remaining showed no improvement. In a 2x2 contingency table of Drug vs Improvement, what is the frequency of patients who received Drug A and showed NO improvement?', '225', '125', '75', '100', 'c', 2, 'Calculate the total number of patients who received Drug A, then find 25% of that subtotal.', 'Total patients receiving Drug A = 60% of 500 = 300. The percentage showing no improvement is 100% - 75% = 25%. Thus, frequency = 25% of 300 = 75. Option A (225) is the number of patients receiving Drug A who DID show improvement (75% of 300). Option B (125) results from 25% of the total 500 patients. Option D (100) results from miscalculating 300 - 200.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0028', 4, 'In a company of 100 employees, 60 own a car, 50 own a bicycle, and 20 own neither. Arranging this data into a 2x2 contingency table (Own Car: Yes/No vs Own Bicycle: Yes/No), how many employees own BOTH a car and a bicycle?', '10', '30', '40', '20', 'b', 2, 'Find the number of employees who own at least one vehicle (100 - 20), then use n(A or B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A and B).', 'Employees owning at least one vehicle = 100 - 20 = 80. Using set addition: n(Car) + n(Bicycle) - n(Both) = 80 ==> 60 + 50 - n(Both) = 80 ==> 110 - n(Both) = 80 ==> n(Both) = 30. Option A (10) results from subtracting 50 from 60. Option B (20) is the number owning neither. Option C (40) results from 100 - 60.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0029', 4, 'In an exam taken by 120 candidates, 70 are Male and 50 are Female. Overall, 80 candidates passed and 40 failed. If 50 Male candidates passed, what is the difference between the number of Male candidates who passed and Female candidates who passed?', '25', '30', '10', '20', 'd', 2, 'Find the number of female candidates who passed (80 - 50), then subtract that from the number of male candidates who passed.', 'Total passed = 80. Male passed = 50, so Female passed = 80 - 50 = 30. The difference between Male passed and Female passed is 50 - 30 = 20. Option B (30) is the number of Female candidates who passed. Option C (10) results from 30 - 20 (difference with female fails). Option D (25) is an arbitrary guess.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0030', 4, 'In a 2x2 contingency table, the marginal row totals are R1 = 60 and R2 = 80, and the marginal column totals are C1 = 50 and C2 = 90. What is the grand total N of the observations?', '140', '150', '280', '100', 'a', 1, 'The grand total N is the sum of all row totals, which also equals the sum of all column totals.', 'Grand total N = R1 + R2 = 60 + 80 = 140. (Also C1 + C2 = 50 + 90 = 140). Option A (150) is an arithmetic error. Option C (280) results from adding row totals and column totals together (140 + 140). Option D (100) results from subtracting 50 from 150.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0031', 4, 'In a 2x2 contingency table with a grand total of N = 200 observations, the first row total is R1 = 80 and the first column total is C1 = 60. What is the expected frequency E11 for the cell in the first row and first column?', '48', '14', '30', '24', 'd', 1, 'Use the expected frequency formula: E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / N.', 'Expected frequency E11 = (R1 * C1) / N = (80 * 60) / 200 = 4800 / 200 = 24. Option A (48) results from forgetting to divide by 200 and dividing by 100 instead. Option C (30) results from using (100 * 60) / 200. Option D (14) results from subtracting (80 - 60) / ... or an arithmetic mistake.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0032', 4, 'A 2x2 contingency table has row totals R1 = 100, R2 = 100 and column totals C1 = 50, C2 = 150, with grand total N = 200. What is the expected frequency E22 for the cell in the second row and second column?', '25', '75', '100', '50', 'b', 1, 'Use the formula E22 = (R2 * C2) / N.', 'E22 = (R2 * C2) / N = (100 * 150) / 200 = 15000 / 200 = 75. Option A (25) is E21 = (100 * 50) / 200. Option B (50) is C1. Option C (100) is R2.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0033', 4, 'The observed frequencies in a 2x2 contingency table are: O11 = 20, O12 = 30, O21 = 30, and O22 = 20, with grand total N = 100. What is the calculated value of the Chi-square statistic (chi^2)?', '1.0', '2.0', '8.0', '4.0', 'd', 2, 'Find the row and column totals (all 50), calculate expected frequency E = 25 for all cells, then sum (O - E)^2 / E.', 'Row totals: R1 = 50, R2 = 50. Column totals: C1 = 50, C2 = 50. Grand total N = 100. Expected frequencies: E_ij = (50 * 50) / 100 = 25 for each cell. For every cell, (O - E)^2 = (20 - 25)^2 = 25. Then chi^2 = 4 * (25 / 25) = 4 * 1 = 4.0. Option B (2.0) results from calculating only two cells. Option C (8.0) results from dividing by (O - E) instead of E. Option D (1.0) results from computing for only a single cell.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0034', 4, 'In a test of independence, a 2x2 contingency table has observed frequencies: O11 = 30, O12 = 30, O21 = 20, and O22 = 20. What is the value of the Chi-square statistic?', '0.0', '10.0', '2.5', '5.0', 'a', 2, 'Compute expected frequencies for each cell and check if observed frequencies equal expected frequencies.', 'Row totals: R1 = 60, R2 = 40. Column totals: C1 = 50, C2 = 50. N = 100. Expected frequencies: E11 = (60*50)/100 = 30, E12 = (60*50)/100 = 30, E21 = (40*50)/100 = 20, E22 = (40*50)/100 = 20. Since O_ij = E_ij for all cells, (O - E) = 0 for every cell, making chi^2 = 0.0. Option A (10.0) results from adding row differences (60 - 50). Option C (2.5) and Option D (5.0) result from incorrect formulas.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0035', 4, 'In a Chi-square computation, a cell has an observed frequency of O = 45 and an expected frequency of E = 36. What is the contribution of this single cell, (O - E)^2 / E, to the Chi-square statistic?', '2.25', '0.25', '1.80', '9.00', 'a', 1, 'Calculate (O - E), square the result, and divide by E.', '(O - E) = 45 - 36 = 9. Squaring gives (9)^2 = 81. Dividing by E: 81 / 36 = 2.25. Option A (0.25) results from (9 / 36) without squaring. Option C (1.80) results from dividing 81 by 45 (dividing by O instead of E). Option D (9.00) results from (O - E) without squaring or dividing.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0036', 4, 'In a survey of 200 individuals on a policy reform, 60 out of 100 Men voted In Favor and 40 voted Against. Among 100 Women, 40 voted In Favor and 60 voted Against. What is the calculated Chi-square statistic?', '4.0', '16.0', '2.0', '8.0', 'd', 2, 'Find the expected frequencies for all 4 cells (each is 50), then compute sum (O - E)^2 / E.', 'Row totals: R1 = 100, R2 = 100. Column totals: C1 = 100, C2 = 100. Grand total N = 200. Expected frequency for all 4 cells: E = (100 * 100) / 200 = 50. For each cell, |O - E| = 10, so (O - E)^2 = 100. Contribution of each cell = 100 / 50 = 2.0. Total chi^2 = 2.0 + 2.0 + 2.0 + 2.0 = 8.0. Option A (4.0) results from summing only 2 cells. Option B (16.0) results from multiplying by 8 instead of 4. Option D (2.0) is the contribution of a single cell.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0037', 4, 'A 2x2 contingency table classifies 100 patients into two treatment groups. Group 1 has 10 Recovered and 40 Not Recovered. Group 2 has 30 Recovered and 20 Not Recovered. What is the value of the Chi-square statistic (to two decimal places)?', '10.00', '16.67', '8.33', '12.50', 'b', 3, 'Calculate expected frequencies E11=20, E12=30, E21=20, E22=30, then sum (O - E)^2 / E.', 'Row totals: R1 = 50, R2 = 50. Column totals: C1 = 40, C2 = 60. N = 100. Expected frequencies: E11 = (50*40)/100 = 20; E12 = (50*60)/100 = 30; E21 = 20; E22 = 30. Cell contributions: (10-20)^2/20 = 100/20 = 5.0; (40-30)^2/30 = 100/30 = 3.33; (30-20)^2/20 = 5.0; (20-30)^2/30 = 3.33. Total chi^2 = 5.0 + 3.33 + 5.0 + 3.33 = 16.67. Option B (10.00) is just the sum of the two equal contributions (5.0 + 5.0). Option C (8.33) is the sum of row 1 contributions only (5 + 3.33). Option D (12.50) results from dividing by 16.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0038', 4, 'Which of the following expressions correctly defines the Chi-square statistic (chi^2) computed from observed frequencies (O) and expected frequencies (E)?', 'Sigma [ (O - E) / E ]', 'Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / O ]', 'Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / E ]', 'Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / N ]', 'c', 1, 'Recall that deviations (O - E) are squared and divided by expected frequencies E.', 'By definition, the Chi-square statistic is given by chi^2 = Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / E ]. Option A is incorrect because the difference (O - E) is not squared. Option B is incorrect because the divisor is the observed frequency O instead of the expected frequency E. Option D is incorrect because the divisor is the grand total N instead of cell expected frequency E.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0039', 4, 'In a 2x2 contingency table, the observed row total for the first row is R1 = 85. What is the sum of the expected frequencies of the cells in this first row, E11 + E12?', '42.5', '85', '170', '0', 'b', 2, 'Consider the mathematical property of expected frequencies when summed across a row.', 'For any row i in a contingency table, the sum of expected frequencies across all columns equals the row total: Sigma_j E_ij = R_i. Hence, E11 + E12 = R1 = 85. Option A (42.5) results from dividing by 2. Option C (170) results from doubling the row total. Option D (0) results from confusing expected frequencies with the sum of deviations (O - E).'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0040', 4, 'In calculating the Chi-square statistic for a 2x2 table, the individual cell values of (O - E)^2 / E are found to be 1.25, 0.75, 1.25, and 0.75. What is the final value of the Chi-square statistic?', '2.00', '8.00', '1.00', '4.00', 'd', 1, 'Add the individual cell contributions together.', 'The Chi-square statistic is the sum of (O - E)^2 / E over all cells: chi^2 = 1.25 + 0.75 + 1.25 + 0.75 = 4.00. Option A (2.00) results from summing only the first row (1.25 + 0.75). Option C (1.00) results from averaging the values (4.00 / 4). Option D (8.00) results from doubling the sum.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0041', 4, 'A 2x3 contingency table classifies 300 employees across 2 shifts and 3 job roles. If the first shift has a row total of R1 = 120 and the second job role has a column total of C2 = 50, what is the expected frequency E12 for that cell?', '20', '30', '25', '40', 'a', 1, 'Apply the formula E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / N with R1 = 120, C2 = 50, and N = 300.', 'E12 = (R1 * C2) / N = (120 * 50) / 300 = 6000 / 300 = 20. Option B (30) results from (120 * 50) / 200. Option C (25) results from (150 * 50) / 300. Option D (40) results from 120 / 3.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0042', 4, 'In a 2x3 contingency table, each of the 2 rows has a total of 90, and each of the 3 columns has a total of 60 (with grand total N = 180). What is the expected frequency for each cell in the table?', '45', '20', '30', '15', 'c', 1, 'Compute E_ij = (90 * 60) / 180.', 'Expected frequency for any cell is E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / N = (90 * 60) / 180 = 5400 / 180 = 30. Option A (45) results from 90 / 2. Option C (20) results from 60 / 3. Option D (15) results from 90 / 6.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0043', 4, 'A 2x3 table records preferences for three elective courses (Arts, Commerce, Science) among 60 Boys and 60 Girls (total N = 120). The observed counts are: Boys: (15, 30, 15) and Girls: (25, 10, 25). Each elective course has a total of 40 students. What is the value of the Chi-square statistic?', '7.5', '12.0', '15.0', '10.0', 'c', 3, 'All expected frequencies are (60 * 40) / 120 = 20. Compute (O - E)^2 / E for each of the 6 cells and sum them.', 'For all 6 cells, E = (60 * 40) / 120 = 20. Cell deviations (O - E) are: Boys: -5, 10, -5; Girls: 5, -10, 5. Squared deviations: 25, 100, 25 for Boys; 25, 100, 25 for Girls. Dividing each by E = 20: 1.25, 5.00, 1.25 for Boys (sum = 7.5); 1.25, 5.00, 1.25 for Girls (sum = 7.5). Total chi^2 = 7.5 + 7.5 = 15.0. Option A (7.5) is the sum for only one row. Option B (12.0) results from miscalculating 100/20 as 4. Option D (10.0) is 5.00 + 5.00.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0044', 4, 'When computing the Chi-square statistic for a 2x3 contingency table, how many individual cell terms of the form (O - E)^2 / E must be calculated and summed?', '5', '8', '6', '2', 'c', 1, 'The number of terms equals the total number of cells in the table (rows * columns).', 'A 2x3 contingency table consists of 2 rows and 3 columns, yielding 2 * 3 = 6 cells. Therefore, exactly 6 terms are computed and summed. Option A (5) confuses the number of cells with degrees of freedom (r-1)*(c-1) + ... Option C (8) results from adding rows and columns plus two (2 + 3 + 3). Option D (2) is only the number of rows.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0045', 4, 'In a 2x3 contingency table with total sample size N = 240, row 2 total is R2 = 80 and column 3 total is C3 = 45. What is the expected frequency E23?', '20', '15', '12', '18', 'b', 1, 'Use E23 = (R2 * C3) / N.', 'E23 = (80 * 45) / 240 = 3600 / 240 = 15. Option A (20) results from (80 * 60) / 240. Option B (12) results from 3600 / 300. Option D (18) results from an arithmetic error.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0046', 4, 'In a 2x3 contingency table where row 2 has total observed frequency R2 = 140, what is the sum of the expected frequencies for the second row, E21 + E22 + E23?', '240', '100', '70', '140', 'd', 1, 'The sum of the expected frequencies across any row always equals that row''s marginal total.', 'By the properties of expected frequencies, Sigma_{j=1}^3 E_2j = R2. Hence, E21 + E22 + E23 = 140. Option B (100) is the total of row 1. Option C (70) results from dividing 140 by 2. Option D (240) is the grand total N.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0047', 4, 'A 2x3 contingency table has row totals R1 = 50, R2 = 50 (N = 100) and column totals C1 = 30, C2 = 40, C3 = 30. The observed frequencies are: Row 1: (15, 25, 10) and Row 2: (15, 15, 20). What is the Chi-square statistic (to two decimal places)?', '5.83', '4.17', '2.92', '7.50', 'a', 3, 'Compute expected frequencies (15, 20, 15) for both rows, then find (O - E)^2 / E for each cell and add them.', 'Expected frequencies are E_1j = E_2j = (50 * C_j)/100 = (15, 20, 15). Deviations (O - E): Row 1: 0, +5, -5; Row 2: 0, -5, +5. Cell contributions: Col 1: 0/15 + 0/15 = 0; Col 2: 25/20 + 25/20 = 1.25 + 1.25 = 2.50; Col 3: 25/15 + 25/15 = 1.667 + 1.667 = 3.333. Total chi^2 = 0 + 2.50 + 3.333 = 5.83. Option B (4.17) results from omitting one cell in Col 3. Option C (2.92) is half of the total (Row 1 only). Option D (7.50) results from dividing all squared deviations by 20.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0048', 4, 'In any contingency table, what is the sum of differences between observed and expected frequencies, Sigma (O - E), taken across all cells in a single row?', 'Grand total N', 'Row total R', '1', '0', 'd', 1, 'Recall that the sum of observed frequencies in a row equals the sum of expected frequencies in that row.', 'For any row i, Sigma_j O_ij = R_i and Sigma_j E_ij = R_i. Therefore, Sigma_j (O_ij - E_ij) = R_i - R_i = 0. Option A is incorrect because deviations balance out to zero, not N. Option B is the sum of O alone. Option D is incorrect.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0049', 4, 'In a 2x3 contingency table, if the observed frequency in every cell is exactly equal to its corresponding expected frequency (O_ij = E_ij for all i, j), what is the value of the Chi-square statistic?', '0', '6', '1', 'Cannot be determined', 'a', 1, 'Substitute O_ij - E_ij = 0 into the Chi-square formula.', 'The Chi-square statistic is chi^2 = Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / E ]. When O_ij = E_ij for all cells, (O_ij - E_ij) = 0 for every cell, making the numerator of every term 0. Hence, chi^2 = 0. Option A (1) confuses zero with unity. Option B (6) is the number of cells. Option D is incorrect because the result is mathematically exact.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0050', 4, 'Four cells in a contingency table have the following pairs of (Observed, Expected) frequencies: Cell A: (20, 20), Cell B: (35, 25), Cell C: (15, 25), and Cell D: (10, 20). Which cell provides the largest contribution to the Chi-square statistic?', 'Cell D', 'Cell C', 'Cell A', 'Cell B', 'a', 2, 'Calculate (O - E)^2 / E for each cell and compare the results.', 'Calculate (O - E)^2 / E for each cell: Cell A: (20 - 20)^2 / 20 = 0; Cell B: (35 - 25)^2 / 25 = 100 / 25 = 4.0; Cell C: (15 - 25)^2 / 25 = 100 / 25 = 4.0; Cell D: (10 - 20)^2 / 20 = 100 / 20 = 5.0. Cell D has the largest contribution (5.0). Option A and Option B have contribution 4.0. Option C has contribution 0.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0051', 4, 'A study that simultaneously observes and records two distinct variables on the same population (such as heights and weights of individuals) generates which type of frequency distribution?', 'Univariate frequency distribution', 'Bivariate frequency distribution', 'Cumulative frequency distribution', 'Relative frequency distribution', 'b', 1, 'Recall the term used for the simultaneous study of two variables observed on the same population.', 'A frequency distribution of two variables observed simultaneously on the same population is called a bivariate frequency distribution. A univariate distribution involves only a single variable (Option A). Cumulative and relative distributions (Options C and D) refer to specific presentation forms of univariate frequencies.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0052', 4, 'Which of the following variables is classified as a categorical variable?', 'Monthly income in rupees', 'Height of a student in centimeters', 'Blood group of a patient (A, B, AB, O)', 'Temperature in degrees Celsius', 'c', 1, 'A categorical variable takes non-numerical, qualitative category values.', 'A variable that takes non-numerical values (such as Blood group: A, B, AB, O, or Gender, or Mother tongue) is called a categorical variable. Height, monthly income, and temperature (Options B, C, D) are quantitative/numerical variables.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0053', 4, 'In statistical methodology, any summary quantity calculated directly from sample data (such as the sample mean or Chi-square value) is formally defined as a:', 'Statistic', 'Parameter', 'Variable', 'Constant', 'a', 1, 'Recall the general definition given for any quantity computed from sample data.', 'In general, any quantity calculated from sample data is called a statistic. A parameter (Option A) is a numerical characteristic of an entire population. A variable (Option C) is the characteristic being measured, and a constant (Option D) does not vary.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0054', 4, 'A two-way frequency table that summarizes the joint frequency distribution of two categorical variables is specifically called a:', 'Frequency polygon', 'Histogram', 'Contingency table', 'Ogive', 'c', 1, 'Recall the specific name given to a bivariate frequency distribution for categorical variables.', 'A bivariate frequency distribution for categorical variables arranged in a two-way table is called a contingency table. Histograms, frequency polygons, and ogives (Options B, C, D) are graphical representations of continuous univariate frequency distributions.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0055', 4, 'If discrete variable X takes m distinct values and variable Y takes n distinct values, how many cells (excluding total rows and columns) are present in the bivariate frequency table?', 'm + n', '2 * (m + n)', '(m - 1) * (n - 1)', 'm * n', 'd', 1, 'The table has m columns and n rows of data cells.', 'A bivariate table with m values of X and n values of Y has m columns and n rows of data, forming exactly m * n cells. Option A (m + n) is the sum. Option C ((m - 1)(n - 1)) represents the degrees of freedom in contingency analysis, not the total number of cells. Option D is the perimeter of a grid.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0056', 4, 'Which of the following statements regarding the Chi-square statistic (chi^2) is always true?', 'chi^2 can take negative values if observed frequencies are smaller than expected frequencies.', 'chi^2 is always strictly greater than the sample size N.', 'chi^2 is always non-negative (chi^2 >= 0).', 'chi^2 must always be an integer.', 'c', 1, 'Examine the squared term (O - E)^2 in the formula and its sign.', 'Because each term in the sum has a squared numerator (O - E)^2 >= 0 and expected frequencies E > 0, every term is non-negative. Consequently, the Chi-square statistic chi^2 is always non-negative (chi^2 >= 0). Option A is false because squaring eliminates negative signs. Options C and D are false as chi^2 can be smaller than N and is generally a decimal.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0057', 4, 'In a bivariate frequency table, the individual frequency distribution of the values of variable X obtained by summing across all values of variable Y is known as the:', 'Marginal frequency distribution of X', 'Joint frequency distribution of X and Y', 'Conditional frequency distribution of X', 'Cumulative frequency distribution of X', 'a', 1, 'Look for the distribution placed in the margin (column/row totals) of the table.', 'The frequency distribution of the values of variable X together with their column/row totals (f_x) is called the marginal frequency distribution of X. A conditional distribution (Option A) restricts attention to a single fixed value of Y. Joint distribution (Option B) refers to the entire table of paired frequencies.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0058', 4, 'The frequency distribution of variable Y obtained when variable X is held fixed at a specific value (e.g. X = x_i) is called the:', 'Marginal frequency distribution of Y', 'Continuous distribution', 'Univariate normal distribution', 'Conditional frequency distribution of Y given X', 'd', 1, 'The distribution depends on a given condition or fixed value of the other variable.', 'The frequency distribution of one variable when the value of the other variable is given/fixed is called a conditional frequency distribution. Marginal distribution (Option A) sums over all values of the other variable rather than fixing one.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0059', 4, 'In a bivariate table with m values of X and n values of Y, if f_i. denotes the marginal total for row i and f_.j denotes the marginal total for column j, which mathematical relationship correctly expresses the grand total N?', 'N = Sigma_{i=1}^m f_i. = Sigma_{j=1}^n f_.j', 'N = Sigma_{i=1}^m f_i. + Sigma_{j=1}^n f_.j', 'N = (Sigma_{i=1}^m f_i.) * (Sigma_{j=1}^n f_.j)', 'N = Sigma_{i=1}^m f_i. - Sigma_{j=1}^n f_.j', 'a', 2, 'The sum of all row totals equals the sum of all column totals, which is the grand total N.', 'The grand total N is obtained by summing all row totals, which identically equals the sum of all column totals: N = Sigma f_i. = Sigma f_.j = Sigma Sigma f_ij. Option B incorrectly doubles N by adding row and column totals together. Options C and D are mathematically invalid.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0060', 4, 'Under the assumption of independence between two categorical variables, what is the formula to compute the expected frequency E_ij for the cell in row i and column j?', 'E_ij = (R_i + C_j) / N', 'E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / (R_i + C_j)', 'E_ij = N / (R_i * C_j)', 'E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / N', 'd', 1, 'Expected frequency is the product of row total and column total divided by grand total.', 'The expected frequency of cell (i, j) in a contingency table is given by E_ij = (R_i * C_j) / N, where R_i is the row total, C_j is the column total, and N is the grand total. Option A adds instead of multiplying. Option C inverts the formula. Option D divides by the sum of margins.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0061', 4, 'In the standard construction of a bivariate frequency distribution table as presented in statistical practice, where are the values/classes of variable X and variable Y conventionally placed?', 'Values of X are placed horizontally at the top, and values of Y vertically on the left side.', 'Values of X and Y are both placed only in the bottom row.', 'Values of X and Y are placed exclusively in the rightmost column.', 'Values of X are placed along the diagonal of the table.', 'a', 1, 'Recall the standard layout where one variable forms the columns across the top and the other forms the rows on the left.', 'Conventionally, a bivariate frequency table is constructed by taking values of variable X horizontally at the top (column headers) and values of variable Y vertically on the left side (row headers). Options B, C, and D describe impossible or non-standard arrangements that do not form a two-way grid.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0062', 4, 'When the calculated Chi-square statistic for a contingency table is exactly zero (chi^2 = 0), what does this indicate about the observed and expected frequencies?', 'Every observed frequency is twice the expected frequency.', 'The observed frequency in every cell is identical to its expected frequency.', 'All observed frequencies are equal to zero.', 'The sample size N must be equal to zero.', 'b', 2, 'If a sum of squared terms is zero, each individual numerator must be zero.', 'Since chi^2 = Sigma [ (O - E)^2 / E ] and each term is non-negative, the sum can only equal zero if every numerator (O_ij - E_ij)^2 = 0, which means O_ij = E_ij for all cells. Option A would yield a strictly positive Chi-square. Option C is impossible for a non-empty sample. Option D is incorrect because N > 0.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0063', 4, 'What is the key structural difference between a univariate frequency table and a bivariate frequency table?', 'A univariate table requires Chi-square calculation, whereas a bivariate table does not.', 'A univariate table has rows and columns, whereas a bivariate table has only a single row.', 'A univariate table summarizes one variable in a single list/column, whereas a bivariate table summarizes two variables simultaneously in a two-way grid.', 'A univariate table can only be constructed for non-numerical variables.', 'c', 1, 'Consider how many variables are classified and whether the table is one-way or two-way.', 'A univariate frequency table summarizes observations on a single variable (one-way classification), whereas a bivariate frequency table summarizes simultaneous observations on two variables in a two-way table. Option B reverses the dimensionalities. Option C is false because Chi-square applies to bivariate contingency tables. Option D is false because univariate tables commonly handle numerical data.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0064', 4, 'In the notation of the Chi-square statistic (chi^2), the symbol ''chi'' (chi) is a letter derived from which alphabet?', 'Greek alphabet', 'Latin alphabet', 'Cyrillic alphabet', 'Hebrew alphabet', 'a', 1, 'Recall the origin of mathematical symbols like alpha, beta, and chi.', '''Chi'' (written as chi and read as ''Kai'' or ''Ki'') is a letter of the Greek alphabet. Options A, C, and D name other alphabets.'),
        ('mth11b-04-mcq-0065', 4, 'In a 2x2 contingency table with fixed marginal totals, how many cell frequencies can be freely chosen before all remaining cell frequencies are completely determined?', '1', '2', '3', '4', 'a', 2, 'Once you fill in a single cell in a 2x2 table with known row and column totals, all other three cells are fixed by subtraction.', 'In a 2x2 contingency table with known marginal totals, once any one cell frequency is specified, the remaining three cell frequencies are automatically determined by subtracting from the corresponding row and column totals. Hence, only 1 cell frequency can be freely chosen (1 degree of freedom). Options B, C, and D are incorrect.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0001', 5, 'A delivery truck travels along a highway. The distance covered X (in km) and the time taken Y (in hours) are recorded as: (50, 1), (100, 2), (150, 3), (200, 4), (250, 5). What is the nature and value of the correlation coefficient between distance and time?', 'r = 1 (Perfect positive correlation)', 'r = 0.5 (Moderate positive correlation)', 'r = -1 (Perfect negative correlation)', 'r = 0 (No correlation)', 'a', 1, 'Examine whether the ratio of change between Y and X is strictly constant and positive.', 'Step-by-step working: The points (50, 1), (100, 2), (150, 3), (200, 4), (250, 5) satisfy the exact linear equation Y = 0.02X with a positive slope. When plotted on a scatter diagram, all points lie exactly on a single straight line rising from left to right. Hence, r = 1 (perfect positive correlation). Distractor analysis: Option B (r = 0.5) results from incorrectly assuming the correlation is imperfect because values increase in large steps; Option C (r = -1) results from confusing positive slope with negative slope; Option D (r = 0) results from misinterpreting a straight line as lack of correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0002', 5, 'The total cost Y (in $) of purchasing identical notebooks is recorded for different quantities X: (4, 12), (8, 24), (12, 36), (16, 48), (20, 60). On a scatter diagram, what pattern do these points form and what is the correlation coefficient?', 'A wide band falling from left to right, r = -0.6', 'A straight line rising from left to right, r = 1', 'A horizontal straight line, r = 0', 'A straight line falling from left to right, r = -1', 'b', 1, 'Check the relation Y = 3X and plot the points mentally.', 'Step-by-step working: Here Y = 3X for all observations. Since every point lies exactly on a line with positive slope m = 3 rising from left to right, the scatter diagram forms a straight line rising from left to right and r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option A results from confusing direct variation with a negative scatter band; Option C results from confusing constant rate of change with zero slope; Option D results from confusing direct proportion with inverse proportion.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0003', 5, 'An electric heater consumes power over time. The energy consumed X (in kWh) and electricity bill component Y (in Rs.) are recorded: (10, 70), (20, 140), (30, 210), (40, 280), (50, 350). What type of correlation exists between energy consumed and electricity cost?', 'Perfect negative correlation', 'Moderate positive correlation', 'Perfect positive correlation', 'No correlation', 'c', 1, 'Observe the rate of increase of Y with respect to X.', 'Step-by-step working: As X increases by 10, Y increases by 70 uniformly (Y = 7X). All points lie precisely on an upward-sloping straight line, representing perfect positive correlation (r = 1). Distractor analysis: Option A results from reversing the direction of change; Option B results from assuming empirical data cannot have perfect correlation; Option C results from confusing uniform change with independence.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0004', 5, 'Water flows into a tank at a steady rate. Depth of water X (in cm) and volume Y (in litres) are recorded: (15, 45), (30, 90), (45, 135), (60, 180), (75, 225). What is the correlation coefficient r between depth and volume?', 'r = -1', 'r = 0.8', 'r = 0', 'r = 1', 'd', 1, 'Check if all points lie exactly on a single straight line.', 'Step-by-step working: Y = 3X for all data points. When all points lie on a straight line rising from left to right, the correlation coefficient is exactly r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option B results from using the high positive category threshold instead of recognizing exact linearity; Option C results from failing to see the linear pattern; Option D results from introducing a negative sign error.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0005', 5, 'A factory records machine running hours X and output units Y: (2, 60), (4, 120), (6, 180), (8, 240), (10, 300). If these pairs are plotted on a scatter diagram, which statement is true?', 'Points lie on a straight line rising from left to right, r = 1', 'Points fall from left to right, r = -1', 'Points are scattered randomly, r = 0', 'Points form a curve, r cannot be determined', 'a', 1, 'Look at the ratio Y/X for each pair.', 'Step-by-step working: Y/X = 30 for every pair. All points lie on the straight line Y = 30X rising from left to right, so r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option A results from assuming real manufacturing data must be randomly scattered; Option B results from reversing the coordinate axes direction; Option D results from mistakenly suspecting non-linearity.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0006', 5, 'The perimeter Y of an equilateral triangle with side length X is recorded for sides 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 cm as (3, 9), (5, 15), (7, 21), (9, 27), (11, 33). What is the correlation coefficient between side length and perimeter?', '0.33', '1.0', '3.0', '0.0', 'b', 1, 'Recall the mathematical definition of perimeter of an equilateral triangle Y = 3X and the range of r.', 'Step-by-step working: Y = 3X is a perfect linear relation with positive slope. Hence r = 1.0. Distractor analysis: Option A results from dividing 1 by the slope 3 (1/3 ≈ 0.33); Option C results from confusing the slope of the line (m = 3) with the correlation coefficient, violating -1 <= r <= 1; Option D results from concluding no correlation due to exact deterministic relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0007', 5, 'A car moves at a uniform speed of 60 km/hr. Time in hours X and distance in km Y are given by (1.5, 90), (2.5, 150), (3.5, 210), (4.5, 270). What is the value of Karl Pearson correlation coefficient r?', '-1', '0', '1', '0.6', 'c', 1, 'Determine whether distance is directly proportional to time at constant speed.', 'Step-by-step working: Distance = 60 * Time. Since Y = 60X has a constant positive slope, all points fall perfectly along a straight line rising from left to right, giving r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option A results from a sign inversion; Option B results from misidentifying uniform motion with zero correlation; Option D results from taking 60/100 as the correlation value.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0008', 5, 'Simple interest Y (in Rs.) earned on a fixed principal at 10% per annum for X years is recorded: (1, 100), (2, 200), (3, 300), (4, 400), (5, 500). What does the scatter diagram show?', 'Zero correlation with r = 0', 'High negative correlation with r = -0.9', 'Moderate positive correlation with r = 0.5', 'Perfect positive correlation with r = 1', 'd', 1, 'Check if Y increases linearly with X at an exact constant rate.', 'Step-by-step working: Y = 100X. In the scatter diagram, all points lie on a straight line rising from left to right, indicating perfect positive correlation, r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option B results from sign error and wrong classification; Option C results from assuming moderate strength due to simple numbers; Option D results from misinterpreting linear predictability.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0009', 5, 'Weight of liquid X (in kg) and its volume Y (in litres) for a substance of density 0.8 kg/L are given as: (8, 10), (16, 20), (24, 30), (32, 40), (40, 50). What is the correlation coefficient between weight and volume?', '1.0', '0.8', '-1.0', '0.0', 'a', 1, 'Distinguish between the physical proportionality constant (slope) and the correlation coefficient r.', 'Step-by-step working: Y = 1.25X. Since the relationship is an exact straight line with positive slope, r = 1.0. Distractor analysis: Option A results from confusing the density value 0.8 with the correlation coefficient; Option C results from a sign confusion; Option D results from assuming density implies no correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0010', 5, 'A worker earns Rs. 250 per day. For days worked X = 4, 8, 12, 16, total wage earned Y = 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000 respectively. Which of the following correctly describes the relationship between days worked and total wage?', 'Points form a straight line falling from left to right, r = -1', 'Points form a straight line rising from left to right, r = 1', 'Points form an irregular band rising from left to right, r = 0.7', 'Points are distributed horizontally, r = 0', 'b', 1, 'Examine whether Y/X is constant.', 'Step-by-step working: Y = 250X. Every point lies on a straight line rising from left to right, which means r = 1. Distractor analysis: Option A results from incorrectly identifying the direction of the line; Option C results from assuming the points scatter into a band instead of a single straight line; Option D results from confusing constant slope with zero slope.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0011', 5, 'A company records advertising expenditure X (in Lakh Rs.) and sales revenue Y (in Crore Rs.): (3, 10), (4, 11), (5, 14), (6, 13), (7, 16), (8, 18). When plotted on a scatter diagram, what type of correlation is observed?', 'Perfect positive correlation', 'Perfect negative correlation', 'Positive correlation', 'Zero correlation', 'c', 1, 'Observe the general direction of the band formed by the plotted points.', 'Step-by-step working: As X increases from 3 to 8, Y generally increases from 10 to 18, but the points do not all fall on a single straight line (e.g. at X=5, Y=14 while at X=6, Y=13). They form a band rising from left to right. Hence, it indicates positive correlation (imperfect positive correlation, r > 0). Distractor analysis: Option A is incorrect because the points do not lie on a single straight line; Option C results from reversing the direction of trend; Option D results from ignoring the upward trend.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0012', 5, 'Study hours per week X and test score Y for 6 students are: (10, 52), (12, 58), (14, 55), (16, 68), (18, 70), (20, 75). On a scatter diagram, the points form a band rising from left to right. What does this indicate?', 'Perfect negative correlation between study hours and test score', 'Negative correlation between study hours and test score', 'Zero correlation between study hours and test score', 'Positive correlation between study hours and test score', 'd', 1, 'Identify the trend: does score tend to rise as study hours increase?', 'Step-by-step working: The points form a band rising from left to right, showing that higher study hours are generally associated with higher test scores. This indicates positive correlation. Distractor analysis: Option B results from confusing rising band with falling band; Option C results from wrongly concluding that because points do not form a single line there is no correlation; Option D results from misidentifying both slope and linearity.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0013', 5, 'Fertilizer applied X (kg/plot) and crop yield Y (quintals) are recorded: (15, 22), (20, 25), (25, 24), (30, 29), (35, 31), (40, 35). A scatter diagram of this bivariate data will show:', 'A band of points rising from left to right', 'A band of points falling from left to right', 'Points falling on a straight line from left to right', 'Points scattered in a vertical line parallel to the Y-axis', 'a', 1, 'Look at how crop yield changes as fertilizer increases.', 'Step-by-step working: As fertilizer X increases from 15 to 40, yield Y generally increases from 22 to 35 with minor fluctuations, forming a band rising from left to right. This indicates positive correlation. Distractor analysis: Option A results from assuming points lie on an exact line; Option B results from reversing the slope direction; Option D results from confusing bivariate variation with a constant X scenario.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0014', 5, 'Monthly family income X (in thousands of Rs.) and monthly savings Y (in thousands of Rs.) are: (30, 4), (40, 7), (50, 6), (60, 10), (70, 11), (80, 15). The scatter diagram shows a band rising from left to right. Which conclusion is correct?', 'Income and savings have negative correlation', 'Income and savings have positive correlation', 'Income and savings have perfect positive correlation (r = 1)', 'Income and savings have zero correlation (r = 0)', 'b', 1, 'Check if the points form a band or an exact single straight line.', 'Step-by-step working: A band rising from left to right indicates positive correlation (0 < r < 1). Since points do not lie on a single line, r is not 1. Distractor analysis: Option A results from mistaking the upward trend for downward; Option C results from failing to notice fluctuations (at X=40, Y=7; at X=50, Y=6); Option D results from incorrectly requiring strict linearity for any correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0015', 5, 'In a scatter diagram, if the plotted points do not lie on a single straight line but form an upward-sloping band from left to right, what can be inferred about the correlation coefficient r?', 'r = 1', 'r < 0', '0 < r < 1', 'r = 0', 'c', 1, 'Recall the interpretation of scatter bands: rising band means positive, but not strictly linear.', 'Step-by-step working: An upward-sloping band from left to right represents positive correlation. Since the points do not all fall on a straight line, r < 1. Therefore, 0 < r < 1. Distractor analysis: Option A is only true if all points lie strictly on a single line; Option B describes a falling band; Option D describes absence of any trend.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0016', 5, 'A researcher plots heights of fathers X and heights of sons Y for 8 pairs: (160, 162), (165, 164), (168, 170), (170, 168), (172, 174), (175, 176), (178, 175), (180, 182). The scatter diagram shows:', 'A downward-sloping straight line', 'A horizontal band of points', 'A circular cluster of points indicating zero correlation', 'A band of points rising from left to right indicating positive correlation', 'd', 1, 'Observe the overall direction of Y as X increases.', 'Step-by-step working: As fathers height increases, sons height generally increases, creating a band rising from left to right. This indicates positive correlation. Distractor analysis: Option A results from confusing positive slope with negative slope; Option B results from assuming no relation between generations; Option D results from ignoring the upward trajectory.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0017', 5, 'The speed of a vehicle X (km/hr) and braking distance Y (m) are plotted on a scatter diagram. If the points form a band rising from left to right, this indicates:', 'Positive correlation between speed and braking distance', 'Negative correlation between speed and braking distance', 'Zero correlation between speed and braking distance', 'Perfect negative correlation between speed and braking distance', 'a', 1, 'A band rising from left to right corresponds to what type of correlation?', 'Step-by-step working: A band rising from left to right signifies that higher values of X correspond to higher values of Y, which is positive correlation. Distractor analysis: Option B results from confusing rising and falling directions; Option C results from assuming curvature implies zero correlation; Option D results from compounding sign and linearity errors.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0018', 5, 'Observations on temperature X (°C) and ice cream sales Y (units) are: (22, 110), (25, 130), (28, 125), (31, 150), (34, 165), (37, 180). Which description best matches the scatter diagram?', 'Points fall on a straight line from left to right', 'Points are clustered along a band rising from left to right, showing positive correlation', 'Points form a vertical line', 'Points form a band falling from left to right, showing negative correlation', 'b', 1, 'Plot the points conceptually: as temperature rises, do sales increase?', 'Step-by-step working: Sales generally rise with temperature, but not along a single line due to slight variation (e.g. 130 then 125). Thus, the points cluster along a band rising from left to right, showing positive correlation. Distractor analysis: Option A ignores the dip at 28°C; Option C is only for constant X; Option D inverts the trend direction.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0019', 5, 'A scatter diagram has points (1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 7), (5, 6), (6, 9). Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation?', 'Negative correlation of high degree', 'Zero correlation', 'Positive correlation', 'Perfect positive correlation', 'c', 1, 'Check if Y increases as X increases, and whether all points are collinear.', 'Step-by-step working: The values of Y increase overall as X increases (3, 5, 4, 7, 6, 9), forming a rising band. Since points do not lie on a single line, it represents positive correlation (not perfect). Distractor analysis: Option A has the wrong sign; Option B ignores the distinct upward trend; Option D incorrectly assumes perfect collinearity.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0020', 5, 'If the width of a band of points rising from left to right in a scatter diagram is relatively narrow (smaller), what does this indicate according to the chapter?', 'Positive correlation of low degree', 'Negative correlation of high degree', 'Perfect negative correlation', 'Positive correlation of high degree', 'd', 1, 'Consider how the width of the band relates to the degree of correlation.', 'Step-by-step working: When the band is rising from left to right, the correlation is positive. If the width of the band is smaller (narrower), the correlation is of high degree. Distractor analysis: Option A confuses smaller width with low degree (larger width means low degree); Option B has the wrong sign; Option D confuses a narrow band with an exact negative line.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0021', 5, 'Given n = 50, x̄ = 40, ȳ = 50, σx = 8, σy = 10, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 3200. Compute the correlation coefficient r.', '0.80', '0.64', '0.40', '0.08', 'a', 2, 'First compute cov(x, y) = (1/n) Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ), then use r = cov(x, y) / (σx * σy).', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 3200 / 50 = 64. Then r = cov(x, y) / (σx * σy) = 64 / (8 * 10) = 64 / 80 = 0.80. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.64) results from dividing cov(x,y) by 100 instead of 80; Option C (0.40) results from dividing by (8+10) or omitting σx; Option D (0.08) results from dividing cov by 800 instead of 80.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0022', 5, 'For a bivariate data set of 80 observations, σx = 5, σy = 6, and the sum of products of deviations from their respective means is 1800. What is the correlation coefficient r?', '0.60', '0.75', '0.90', '0.45', 'b', 2, 'Divide the sum of products of deviations by n to obtain cov(x, y), then divide by the product of standard deviations.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 1800 / 80 = 22.5. r = cov(x, y) / (σx * σy) = 22.5 / (5 * 6) = 22.5 / 30 = 0.75. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.60) results from dividing 1800 by (80 * 5 * 6) with an arithmetic slip (1800/3000=0.60, forgetting 80 vs n); Option C (0.90) results from using n=60; Option D (0.45) results from squaring the denominator terms incorrectly.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0023', 5, 'For n = 40 pairs of observations, σx = 4, σy = 5, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = -480. What is the correlation coefficient r?', '0.60', '-0.24', '-0.60', '-0.12', 'c', 2, 'Remember that covariance and correlation coefficient preserve the negative sign.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = -480 / 40 = -12. Then r = cov(x, y) / (σx * σy) = -12 / (4 * 5) = -12 / 20 = -0.60. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.60) results from dropping the negative sign; Option C (-0.24) results from dividing -480 by (40 * 50); Option D (-0.12) results from dividing cov(-12) by 100 instead of 20.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0024', 5, 'From a sample of 20 pairs of items, standard deviations of X and Y are 3 and 7 respectively. If Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 378, find the correlation coefficient.', '1.89', '0.85', '0.63', '0.90', 'd', 2, 'Compute covariance first: cov(x, y) = 378 / 20.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 378 / 20 = 18.9. r = cov(x, y) / (σx * σy) = 18.9 / (3 * 7) = 18.9 / 21 = 0.90. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.85) is an estimation error; Option C (0.63) results from dividing by (20 + 3*7); Option D (1.89) results from failing to divide by (σx * σy) = 21 (just reporting cov/10), which violates -1 <= r <= 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0025', 5, 'Given n = 25, σx = 6, σy = 8, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 600. What is the value of Karl Pearson correlation coefficient?', '0.50', '0.48', '0.24', '0.75', 'a', 2, 'Use the formula r = [ (1/n) Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) ] / (σx * σy).', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 600 / 25 = 24. r = 24 / (6 * 8) = 24 / 48 = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.24) results from dividing 600 / 2500; Option B (0.48) results from using the product σx * σy = 48 as the numerator; Option D (0.75) results from using n=16 instead of 25.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0026', 5, 'A statistical study of 100 industrial firms gives σx = 15, σy = 20, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 21000. Calculate the correlation coefficient r.', '0.35', '0.70', '0.50', '0.84', 'b', 2, 'Calculate covariance cov(x, y) = Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) / n, then divide by σx * σy.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 21000 / 100 = 210. r = 210 / (15 * 20) = 210 / 300 = 0.70. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.35) results from doubling the denominator; Option C (0.50) results from dividing 210 by (15+20)^2 / 35; Option D (0.84) results from using n=80 instead of 100.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0027', 5, 'Two series of 50 items each have standard deviations 4.8 and 3.5 respectively. If the sum of products of deviations from their arithmetic means is 420, find the correlation coefficient between them.', '0.25', '0.75', '0.50', '0.84', 'c', 2, 'Find cov(x, y) = 420 / 50, then divide by (4.8 * 3.5).', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 420 / 50 = 8.4. Product of standard deviations = 4.8 * 3.5 = 16.8. r = 8.4 / 16.8 = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.25) results from squaring the result or doubling denominator; Option C (0.75) is a calculation guess; Option D (0.84) results from omitting division by 16.8 and taking 8.4/10.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0028', 5, 'Given n = 10, σx = 2, σy = 5, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = -80. What is the value and interpretation of the correlation coefficient?', 'r = 0.80, high positive correlation', 'r = -0.40, moderate negative correlation', 'r = -0.08, insignificant correlation', 'r = -0.80, high negative correlation', 'd', 2, 'Be careful with the negative sign: cov = -80/10.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = -80 / 10 = -8. r = -8 / (2 * 5) = -8 / 10 = -0.80. Since r = -0.80, there is a high negative correlation. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.80) results from omitting the negative sign; Option B (-0.40) results from doubling the denominator (2*5*2); Option D (-0.08) results from dividing by 100.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0029', 5, 'For 60 pairs of data, the standard deviations of X and Y are 12 and 15 respectively. If Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 3240, what is the correlation coefficient r?', '0.30', '0.18', '0.54', '0.60', 'a', 2, 'Divide 3240 by 60 to get cov(x, y), then divide by 180.', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 3240 / 60 = 54. r = 54 / (12 * 15) = 54 / 180 = 0.30. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.18) results from confusing 180 with the numerator; Option C (0.54) results from dividing 54 by 100; Option D (0.60) results from dividing 3240 by (60 * 90).'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0030', 5, 'Given n = 15, x̄ = 25, ȳ = 18, σx = 3.01, σy = 3.03, and Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 122. Find the correlation coefficient r (rounded to two decimal places).', '0.45', '0.89', '0.67', '1.22', 'b', 2, 'Calculate cov(x, y) = 122 / 15 and divide by (3.01 * 3.03).', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 122 / 15 = 8.1333. Denominator = σx * σy = 3.01 * 3.03 = 9.1203. r = 8.1333 / 9.1203 ≈ 0.89. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.45) results from dividing by 15 twice; Option B (0.67) is the answer from Example 3 copied by mistake; Option D (1.22) results from forgetting to divide by 15 in the covariance (122 / 9.12 ≈ 13.3) or dropping decimal.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0031', 5, 'Find the correlation coefficient r between X and Y for the data: X: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], Y: [2, 4, 5, 8, 11]. [Given: √2 = 1.414, √10 = 3.162]', '0.78', '0.55', '0.98', '0.44', 'c', 3, 'Compute Σx, Σy, Σx², Σy², Σxy, find means, standard deviations, and covariance.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. Σx = 15, Σy = 30 => x̄ = 3, ȳ = 6. Σx² = 55, Σy² = 230, Σxy = 112. cov(x, y) = (112/5) - (3)(6) = 22.4 - 18 = 4.4. σx = √(55/5 - 3²) = √(11 - 9) = √2 ≈ 1.4142. σy = √(230/5 - 6²) = √(46 - 36) = √10 ≈ 3.1623. r = 4.4 / (1.4142 * 3.1623) = 4.4 / √20 = 4.4 / 4.4721 ≈ 0.9839 ≈ 0.98. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.78) results from using σy²=16; Option C (0.55) results from dividing cov by (x̄ * ȳ) instead of σx * σy; Option D (0.44) results from dividing cov by 10.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0032', 5, 'Calculate the correlation coefficient r between X and Y: X: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10], Y: [5, 7, 9, 11, 13].', '0.50', '0.80', '0.00', '1.00', 'd', 2, 'Notice that Y = X + 3 for all pairs, or apply the standard formula.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. Σx = 30, x̄ = 6. Σy = 45, ȳ = 9. Σx² = 220, σx = √(220/5 - 36) = √8. Σy² = 445, σy = √(445/5 - 81) = √8. Σxy = 310, cov(x, y) = 310/5 - 54 = 62 - 54 = 8. r = 8 / (√8 * √8) = 8 / 8 = 1.00. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.50) results from dividing cov by (σx² + σy²); Option B (0.80) is an arbitrary guess; Option D (0.00) results from assuming addition of 3 removes correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0033', 5, 'Find the correlation coefficient r for the bivariate data: X: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], Y: [10, 8, 6, 4, 2].', '-1.00', '1.00', '-0.50', '0.00', 'a', 2, 'Check the relation Y = 12 - 2X or calculate covariance and standard deviations.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. x̄ = 3, ȳ = 6. Σxy = 1(10)+2(8)+3(6)+4(4)+5(2) = 10+16+18+16+10 = 70. cov(x, y) = 70/5 - 3(6) = 14 - 18 = -4. σx = √(55/5 - 9) = √2. σy = √(220/5 - 36) = √(44 - 36) = √8. r = -4 / (√2 * √8) = -4 / √16 = -4 / 4 = -1.00. Distractor analysis: Option A (1.00) results from dropping the negative sign; Option C (-0.50) results from doubling the denominator; Option D (0.00) results from confusing negative slope with zero slope.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0034', 5, 'Calculate Karl Pearson correlation coefficient for the observations: X: [3, 5, 7, 9, 11], Y: [6, 8, 10, 14, 17]. [Given: √2 = 1.414]', '0.75', '0.99', '0.62', '0.45', 'b', 3, 'Calculate means x̄ = 7, ȳ = 11, then compute σx, σy, and cov(x, y).', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. Σx = 35 => x̄ = 7. Σy = 55 => ȳ = 11. Σx² = 9+25+49+81+121 = 285. σx = √(285/5 - 49) = √(57 - 49) = √8 = 2√2. Σy² = 36+64+100+196+289 = 685. σy = √(685/5 - 121) = √(137 - 121) = √16 = 4. Σxy = 18+40+70+126+187 = 441. cov(x, y) = 441/5 - 77 = 88.2 - 77 = 11.2. r = 11.2 / (2√2 * 4) = 11.2 / (8 * 1.4142) = 11.2 / 11.3137 ≈ 0.9899 ≈ 0.99. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.75) results from using σy = 5 instead of 4; Option C (0.62) results from dividing cov by (x̄ + ȳ); Option D (0.45) results from forgetting the factor 2 in 2√2.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0035', 5, 'Find the correlation coefficient r between X and Y: X: [2, 3, 4, 5, 6], Y: [7, 6, 5, 4, 3].', '-0.80', '1.00', '-1.00', '0.00', 'c', 2, 'Observe the inverse linear relationship Y = 9 - X.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. x̄ = 4, ȳ = 5. Σxy = 14+18+20+20+18 = 90. cov(x, y) = 90/5 - (4)(5) = 18 - 20 = -2. σx = √(90/5 - 16) = √2. σy = √(135/5 - 25) = √2. r = -2 / (√2 * √2) = -2 / 2 = -1.00. Distractor analysis: Option B (1.00) results from omitting the negative sign; Option C (-0.80) is an arbitrary distractor; Option D (0.00) results from mistakenly thinking opposite values cancel to zero.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0036', 5, 'For the data X: [1, 3, 5, 7, 9], Y: [12, 10, 8, 6, 4], determine the correlation coefficient.', '0.50', '-0.50', '1.00', '-1.00', 'd', 2, 'Notice that Y decreases by 2 whenever X increases by 2.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 5. x̄ = 5, ȳ = 8. Σxy = 12+30+40+42+36 = 160. cov(x, y) = 160/5 - 40 = 32 - 40 = -8. σx = √(165/5 - 25) = √8. σy = √(360/5 - 64) = √8. r = -8 / (√8 * √8) = -8 / 8 = -1.00. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.50) results from dropping the minus sign and dividing by 16; Option B (-0.50) results from dividing by 16 instead of 8; Option D (1.00) results from omitting the negative sign.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0037', 5, 'Calculate the correlation coefficient for the data: X: [2, 4, 6, 8], Y: [3, 5, 7, 9].', '1.00', '0.75', '0.50', '-1.00', 'a', 2, 'Check if Y = X + 1.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 4. x̄ = 5, ȳ = 6. Σxy = 6+20+42+72 = 140. cov(x, y) = 140/4 - 30 = 35 - 30 = 5. σx = √(120/4 - 25) = √5. σy = √(164/4 - 36) = √5. r = 5 / (√5 * √5) = 5 / 5 = 1.00. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.75) results from using n-1 in the denominator; Option C (0.50) results from dividing cov by (σx + σy); Option D (-1.00) results from a sign error.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0038', 5, 'For the dataset X: [10, 20, 30], Y: [15, 25, 35], find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.5', '1.0', '0.0', '-1.0', 'b', 1, 'Note that Y = X + 5 for each pair.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 3. x̄ = 20, ȳ = 25. The points lie on the line Y = X + 5 with positive slope. All points are perfectly collinear, so r = 1.0. Distractor analysis: Option B results from assuming small sample size halves the correlation; Option C results from confusing linear shift with zero relation; Option D results from a sign reversal.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0039', 5, 'Compute r for X: [4, 6, 8, 10], Y: [12, 10, 8, 6].', '1.0', '0.0', '-1.0', '-0.5', 'c', 2, 'Notice that Y = 16 - X.', 'Step-by-step working: n = 4. x̄ = 7, ȳ = 9. Σxy = 48+60+64+60 = 232. cov(x, y) = 232/4 - 63 = 58 - 63 = -5. σx = √(216/4 - 49) = √(54 - 49) = √5. σy = √(344/4 - 81) = √(86 - 81) = √5. r = -5 / 5 = -1.0. Distractor analysis: Option A (1.0) results from dropping the minus sign; Option B (0.0) results from thinking equal and opposite values cancel to zero; Option D (-0.5) results from dividing -5 by 10.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0040', 5, 'Find Karl Pearson correlation coefficient for X: [5, 10, 15], Y: [20, 40, 60].', '0.5', '4.0', '2.0', '1.0', 'd', 1, 'Notice that Y = 4X is a straight line through the origin.', 'Step-by-step working: Since Y = 4X for all points, they lie exactly on a line with positive slope. Therefore, r = 1.0. Distractor analysis: Option A results from dividing by 2; Option C (2.0) and Option D (4.0) result from taking the slope or ratio Y/X as r, violating the property that |r| <= 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0041', 5, 'From a bivariate data of 10 pairs: Σx = 120, Σy = 140, Σ(x - 10)² = 200, Σ(y - 12)² = 290, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 12) = 190. Calculate the correlation coefficient r.', '0.75', '0.60', '0.85', '0.95', 'a', 3, 'Define u = x - 10, v = y - 12. Find Σu, Σv, ū, v̄, σu, σv, cov(u,v), and r = cov(u,v) / (σu * σv).', 'Step-by-step working: Let u = x - 10, v = y - 12. Σu = Σx - 10n = 120 - 100 = 20 => ū = 20/10 = 2. Σv = Σy - 12n = 140 - 120 = 20 => v̄ = 20/10 = 2. σu = √(Σu²/n - ū²) = √(200/10 - 2²) = √(20 - 4) = √16 = 4. σv = √(Σv²/n - v̄²) = √(290/10 - 2²) = √(29 - 4) = √25 = 5. cov(u, v) = (1/n)Σuv - ū·v̄ = 190/10 - (2)(2) = 19 - 4 = 15. r_xy = r_uv = cov(u,v) / (σu * σv) = 15 / (4 * 5) = 15 / 20 = 0.75. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.60) results from dividing cov(15) by 25; Option C (0.85) results from forgetting to subtract ū·v̄ = 4 from 19 (19/20 = 0.95) or arithmetic slip; Option D (0.95) results from using 19/20 without subtracting the product of means.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0042', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 150, Σy = 160, Σ(x - 12)² = 340, Σ(y - 15)² = 170, and Σ(x - 12)(y - 15) = 150. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.75', '0.60', '0.50', '0.30', 'b', 3, 'Set u = x - 12, v = y - 15, then evaluate ū, v̄, σu, σv, and cov(u,v).', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 12, v = y - 15. Σu = 150 - 12(10) = 30 => ū = 3. Σv = 160 - 15(10) = 10 => v̄ = 1. σu = √(340/10 - 3²) = √(34 - 9) = √25 = 5. σv = √(170/10 - 1²) = √(17 - 1) = √16 = 4. cov(u, v) = 150/10 - (3)(1) = 15 - 3 = 12. r = cov / (σu * σv) = 12 / (5 * 4) = 12 / 20 = 0.60. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.75) results from using 15/20 (forgetting to subtract ū·v̄); Option C (0.50) results from dividing 12 by (5+4) or wrong denominator; Option D (0.30) results from dividing 12 by 40.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0043', 5, 'For 10 observations, Σx = 110, Σy = 220, Σ(x - 10)² = 100, Σ(y - 20)² = 290, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 20) = 140. What is the value of r?', '0.40', '0.93', '0.80', '0.60', 'c', 3, 'Use change of origin u = x - 10, v = y - 20.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 20. Σu = 110 - 100 = 10 => ū = 1. Σv = 220 - 200 = 20 => v̄ = 2. σu = √(100/10 - 1²) = √9 = 3. σv = √(290/10 - 2²) = √25 = 5. cov(u,v) = 140/10 - 1(2) = 14 - 2 = 12. r = 12 / (3 * 5) = 12 / 15 = 0.80. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.93) results from 14/15 (omitting subtraction of ū·v̄); Option C (0.40) results from dividing by 30; Option D (0.60) results from taking σu=4.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0044', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 100, Σy = 150, Σ(x - 10)² = 180, Σ(y - 15)² = 500, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 15) = 60. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.30', '0.60', '0.06', '0.20', 'd', 3, 'Note that x̄ = 10 and ȳ = 15, so ū = 0 and v̄ = 0.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 15. Since Σx = 100, ū = (100 - 100)/10 = 0. Since Σy = 150, v̄ = (150 - 150)/10 = 0. σu = √(180/10 - 0) = √18. σv = √(500/10 - 0) = √50. cov(u,v) = 60/10 - 0 = 6. σu * σv = √(18 * 50) = √900 = 30. r = 6 / 30 = 0.20. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.60) is the answer from the textbook Example 5 where ū was 4; Option C (0.06) results from dividing 6 by 100; Option D (0.30) results from dividing 6 by 20.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0045', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 140, Σy = 170, Σ(x - 10)² = 250, Σ(y - 15)² = 290, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 15) = 170. Calculate the correlation coefficient r.', '0.60', '0.80', '0.90', '1.13', 'a', 3, 'Define u = x - 10, v = y - 15, then compute ū, v̄, σu, σv, and cov(u,v).', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 15. Σu = 140 - 100 = 40 => ū = 4. Σv = 170 - 150 = 20 => v̄ = 2. σu = √(250/10 - 4²) = √(25 - 16) = √9 = 3. σv = √(290/10 - 2²) = √(29 - 4) = √25 = 5. cov(u, v) = 170/10 - (4)(2) = 17 - 8 = 9. r = 9 / (3 * 5) = 9 / 15 = 0.60. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.80) results from an arithmetic slip in cov; Option C (0.90) results from omitting the subtraction of 8 from 17 (17/15 ≈ 1.13, capped at 0.90); Option D (1.13) results from taking 17/15 directly, violating |r| <= 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0046', 5, 'For n = 10, Σx = 130, Σy = 140, Σ(x - 10)² = 250, Σ(y - 10)² = 410, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 10) = 200. Find the correlation coefficient.', '0.50', '0.40', '1.00', '0.20', 'b', 3, 'Use u = x - 10, v = y - 10 to determine cov(u,v) and standard deviations.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 10. Σu = 130 - 100 = 30 => ū = 3. Σv = 140 - 100 = 40 => v̄ = 4. σu = √(250/10 - 3²) = √(25 - 9) = √16 = 4. σv = √(410/10 - 4²) = √(41 - 16) = √25 = 5. cov(u, v) = 200/10 - (3)(4) = 20 - 12 = 8. r = 8 / (4 * 5) = 8 / 20 = 0.40. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.50) results from dividing 8 by 16; Option C (1.00) results from using 20/20 (omitting product of means); Option D (0.20) results from dividing 8 by 40.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0047', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 120, Σy = 130, Σ(x - 10)² = 130, Σ(y - 10)² = 250, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 10) = 120. Calculate r.', '0.75', '0.60', '0.50', '1.00', 'c', 3, 'Shift origin by 10 for both variables: u = x - 10, v = y - 10.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 10. Σu = 120 - 100 = 20 => ū = 2. Σv = 130 - 100 = 30 => v̄ = 3. σu = √(130/10 - 2²) = √(13 - 4) = √9 = 3. σv = √(250/10 - 3²) = √(25 - 9) = √16 = 4. cov(u,v) = 120/10 - (2)(3) = 12 - 6 = 6. r = 6 / (3 * 4) = 6 / 12 = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.60) results from using σu=2.5; Option C (0.75) results from using cov = 9; Option D (1.00) results from taking 12/12 without subtracting ū·v̄.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0048', 5, 'For n = 10, Σx = 160, Σy = 210, Σ(x - 15)² = 170, Σ(y - 20)² = 100, and Σ(x - 15)(y - 20) = 106. Find r.', '0.48', '0.88', '0.64', '0.80', 'd', 3, 'Let u = x - 15 and v = y - 20, then find the transformed statistics.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 15, v = y - 20. Σu = 160 - 150 = 10 => ū = 1. Σv = 210 - 200 = 10 => v̄ = 1. σu = √(170/10 - 1²) = √16 = 4. σv = √(100/10 - 1²) = √9 = 3. cov(u,v) = 106/10 - (1)(1) = 10.6 - 1 = 9.6. r = 9.6 / (4 * 3) = 9.6 / 12 = 0.80. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.88) results from omitting the subtraction of 1 (10.6/12 ≈ 0.88); Option C (0.64) results from squaring 0.80; Option D (0.48) results from dividing 9.6 by 20.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0049', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 140, Σy = 180, Σ(x - 10)² = 250, Σ(y - 15)² = 340, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 15) = 120. What is the value of the correlation coefficient r?', '0.00', '0.80', '0.60', '0.40', 'a', 3, 'Check the value of covariance: cov(u,v) = (1/n)Σuv - ū·v̄.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 15. Σu = 140 - 100 = 40 => ū = 4. Σv = 180 - 150 = 30 => v̄ = 3. cov(u,v) = 120/10 - (4)(3) = 12 - 12 = 0. Since covariance is zero, r = 0 / (σu * σv) = 0.00. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.80) results from taking 12 / 15 (forgetting to subtract 12); Option C (0.60) is an estimation error; Option D (0.40) results from arbitrary guessing.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0050', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 110, Σy = 120, Σ(x - 10)² = 100, Σ(y - 10)² = 130, and Σ(x - 10)(y - 10) = -25. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.50', '-0.50', '-0.25', '-0.75', 'b', 3, 'Calculate covariance cov(u,v) and remember that Σuv is negative.', 'Step-by-step working: u = x - 10, v = y - 10. Σu = 110 - 100 = 10 => ū = 1. Σv = 120 - 100 = 20 => v̄ = 2. σu = √(100/10 - 1²) = √9 = 3. σv = √(130/10 - 2²) = √9 = 3. cov(u,v) = -25/10 - (1)(2) = -2.5 - 2 = -4.5. r = -4.5 / (3 * 3) = -4.5 / 9 = -0.50. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.50) results from dropping the minus sign; Option C (-0.25) results from using -2.5 / 9 without subtracting 2; Option D (-0.75) results from adding 2 instead of subtracting in the numerator.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0051', 5, 'For a bivariate dataset, n = 25, Σx = 50, Σy = 75, Σx² = 325, Σy² = 625, and Σxy = 300. Calculate the correlation coefficient r.', '0.75', '0.25', '0.50', '1.00', 'c', 2, 'Find means x̄ and ȳ, then compute σx, σy, cov(x,y), and r.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 50 / 25 = 2. ȳ = 75 / 25 = 3. σx = √(325/25 - 2²) = √(13 - 4) = √9 = 3. σy = √(625/25 - 3²) = √(25 - 9) = √16 = 4. cov(x, y) = 300/25 - (2)(3) = 12 - 6 = 6. r = cov / (σx * σy) = 6 / (3 * 4) = 6 / 12 = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.25) results from squaring 0.50; Option C (0.75) results from taking (12 - 3) / 12; Option D (1.00) results from taking 12/12 (forgetting to subtract x̄ȳ).'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0052', 5, 'Given n = 25, Σx = 100, Σy = 125, Σx² = 800, Σy² = 1250, and Σxy = 700. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.56', '0.20', '0.80', '0.40', 'd', 2, 'Determine x̄, ȳ, σx, σy, and cov(x, y).', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 100/25 = 4, ȳ = 125/25 = 5. σx = √(800/25 - 16) = √16 = 4. σy = √(1250/25 - 25) = √25 = 5. cov(x, y) = 700/25 - 20 = 28 - 20 = 8. r = 8 / (4 * 5) = 8 / 20 = 0.40. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.20) results from dividing 8 by 40; Option C (0.80) results from using 16/20; Option D (0.56) results from taking 28 / (4 * 5) = 1.4 or 28/50.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0053', 5, 'For 20 pairs of observations: Σx = 60, Σy = 80, Σx² = 260, Σy² = 500, Σxy = 300. Compute the value of r.', '0.50', '0.25', '0.60', '0.75', 'a', 2, 'Use r = cov(x,y) / (σx * σy).', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 60/20 = 3, ȳ = 80/20 = 4. σx = √(260/20 - 9) = √4 = 2. σy = √(500/20 - 16) = √9 = 3. cov(x, y) = 300/20 - 12 = 15 - 12 = 3. r = 3 / (2 * 3) = 3 / 6 = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.25) results from squaring 0.50; Option C (0.60) results from dividing 3 by 5; Option D (0.75) results from using 15 / 20.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0054', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 50, Σy = 60, Σx² = 340, Σy² = 520, and Σxy = 420. Find r.', '0.70', '1.00', '0.50', '0.85', 'b', 2, 'Calculate the product of standard deviations and compare with covariance.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 50/10 = 5, ȳ = 60/10 = 6. σx = √(340/10 - 25) = √9 = 3. σy = √(520/10 - 36) = √16 = 4. cov(x, y) = 420/10 - 30 = 42 - 30 = 12. r = 12 / (3 * 4) = 12 / 12 = 1.00. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.70) results from using σy=5; Option C (0.50) results from dividing 12 by 24; Option D (0.85) is an estimation error.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0055', 5, 'For n = 10, Σx = 40, Σy = 50, Σx² = 250, Σy² = 500, Σxy = 110. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '-0.40', '0.60', '-0.60', '-0.73', 'c', 2, 'Note the sign of cov(x,y) = Σxy/n - x̄ȳ.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 40/10 = 4, ȳ = 50/10 = 5. σx = √(250/10 - 16) = √9 = 3. σy = √(500/10 - 25) = √25 = 5. cov(x, y) = 110/10 - (4)(5) = 11 - 20 = -9. r = -9 / (3 * 5) = -9 / 15 = -0.60. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.60) results from dropping the negative sign; Option C (-0.40) results from using denominator 22.5; Option D (-0.73) results from taking 11/15.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0056', 5, 'Given n = 50, Σx = 150, Σy = 200, Σx² = 500, Σy² = 1000, and Σxy = 650. What is the value of r?', '1.00', '0.25', '0.65', '0.50', 'd', 2, 'Compute x̄ = 3, ȳ = 4, σx = 1, σy = 2, and cov = 1.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 150/50 = 3, ȳ = 200/50 = 4. σx = √(500/50 - 9) = √1 = 1. σy = √(1000/50 - 16) = √4 = 2. cov(x, y) = 650/50 - (3)(4) = 13 - 12 = 1. r = 1 / (1 * 2) = 0.50. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.25) results from squaring 0.50; Option C (0.65) results from taking 650/1000; Option D (1.00) results from confusing covariance with product of standard deviations.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0057', 5, 'For 20 items, Σx = 40, Σy = 60, Σx² = 260, Σy² = 500, Σxy = 216. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '0.40', '0.30', '0.50', '0.80', 'a', 2, 'Find cov(x,y) = Σxy/n - x̄ȳ and divide by σx·σy.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 40/20 = 2, ȳ = 60/20 = 3. σx = √(260/20 - 4) = √9 = 3. σy = √(500/20 - 9) = √16 = 4. cov(x, y) = 216/20 - (2)(3) = 10.8 - 6 = 4.8. r = 4.8 / (3 * 4) = 4.8 / 12 = 0.40. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.30) results from using σy=5; Option C (0.50) results from taking 6/12; Option D (0.80) results from taking 4.8 / 6.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0058', 5, 'Given n = 10, Σx = 30, Σy = 40, Σx² = 250, Σy² = 250, and Σxy = 240. What is the value of r?', '0.50', '1.00', '0.80', '0.00', 'b', 2, 'Compare cov(x,y) with the maximum possible value σx·σy.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 30/10 = 3, ȳ = 40/10 = 4. σx = √(250/10 - 9) = √16 = 4. σy = √(250/10 - 16) = √9 = 3. cov(x, y) = 240/10 - 12 = 24 - 12 = 12. r = 12 / (4 * 3) = 12 / 12 = 1.00. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.50) results from dividing 12 by 24; Option C (0.80) is an estimation error; Option D (0.00) results from confusing identical sums of squares with zero correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0059', 5, 'Given n = 25, Σx = 125, Σy = 150, Σx² = 850, Σy² = 1525, and Σxy = 900. Calculate r.', '0.60', '0.24', '0.40', '0.72', 'c', 2, 'Follow standard steps: means, standard deviations, covariance, then r.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 125/25 = 5, ȳ = 150/25 = 6. σx = √(850/25 - 25) = √(34 - 25) = √9 = 3. σy = √(1525/25 - 36) = √(61 - 36) = √25 = 5. cov(x, y) = 900/25 - 30 = 36 - 30 = 6. r = 6 / (3 * 5) = 6 / 15 = 0.40. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.24) results from dividing 6 by 25; Option C (0.60) results from taking 6/10; Option D (0.72) results from using 36/50.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0060', 5, 'For 10 pairs: Σx = 20, Σy = 30, Σx² = 130, Σy² = 250, and Σxy = 12. Find the correlation coefficient r.', '-0.60', '0.40', '-0.20', '-0.40', 'd', 2, 'Check if the covariance is negative: cov = Σxy/n - x̄ȳ.', 'Step-by-step working: x̄ = 20/10 = 2, ȳ = 30/10 = 3. σx = √(130/10 - 4) = √9 = 3. σy = √(250/10 - 9) = √16 = 4. cov(x, y) = 12/10 - 6 = 1.2 - 6 = -4.8. r = -4.8 / (3 * 4) = -4.8 / 12 = -0.40. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.40) results from dropping the minus sign; Option C (-0.20) results from dividing -4.8 by 24; Option D (-0.60) results from taking -4.8 / 8.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0061', 5, 'Calculate the correlation coefficient r between X and Y using change of origin: X: [12, 14, 16, 18, 20], Y: [22, 24, 26, 28, 30].', '1.00', '0.50', '-1.00', '0.00', 'a', 2, 'Take origin a = 16 for X and b = 26 for Y, then u = x - 16 and v = y - 26.', 'Step-by-step working: Let u = x - 16 and v = y - 26. Then u values are [-4, -2, 0, 2, 4] and v values are [-4, -2, 0, 2, 4]. Since u = v for all pairs, r_uv = 1.00. Since r_xy = r_uv under shift of origin, r_xy = 1.00. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.50) results from arbitrary guessing; Option C (-1.00) results from a sign reversal; Option D (0.00) results from assuming subtracting means results in zero correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0062', 5, 'Using change of origin u = x - 50 and v = y - 60, a statistician obtains Σu = 0, Σv = 0, Σu² = 100, Σv² = 100, and Σuv = 80 for n = 10 pairs. What is the correlation coefficient r_xy?', '0.64', '0.80', '0.40', '0.08', 'b', 2, 'Since origin shifts do not change r, calculate r_uv directly.', 'Step-by-step working: ū = 0, v̄ = 0. σu = √(100/10) = √10. σv = √(100/10) = √10. cov(u,v) = 80/10 = 8. r_uv = 8 / (√10 * √10) = 8 / 10 = 0.80. Since change of origin does not change the correlation coefficient, r_xy = r_uv = 0.80. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.64) results from squaring 0.80; Option C (0.40) results from dividing by 20; Option D (0.08) results from dividing by 100.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0063', 5, 'For n = 10 observations, let u = x - 30 and v = y - 40. Given Σu = 0, Σv = 0, Σu² = 200, Σv² = 50, and Σuv = -90. Find the correlation coefficient r_xy.', '-0.45', '0.90', '-0.90', '-0.18', 'c', 2, 'Apply r_uv = Σuv / √(Σu² * Σv²) when means are zero.', 'Step-by-step working: When ū = 0 and v̄ = 0, r_uv = Σuv / √(Σu² * Σv²) = -90 / √(200 * 50) = -90 / √10000 = -90 / 100 = -0.90. By change of origin property, r_xy = r_uv = -0.90. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.90) results from dropping the minus sign; Option C (-0.45) results from doubling the denominator; Option D (-0.18) results from dividing by (200+50).'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0064', 5, 'A study on heights of husbands X (in cm) and wives Y (in cm) uses u = x - 170 and v = y - 160. If n = 10, Σu = 0, Σv = 0, Σu² = 90, Σv² = 160, and Σuv = 72, what is r_xy?', '0.50', '0.36', '0.72', '0.60', 'd', 2, 'Use r = Σuv / √(Σu² * Σv²).', 'Step-by-step working: r_xy = r_uv = 72 / √(90 * 160) = 72 / √14400 = 72 / 120 = 0.60. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.36) results from squaring 0.60; Option C (0.72) results from dividing 72 by 100; Option D (0.50) results from dividing 72 by (90+160)/2 = 125 or guessing.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0065', 5, 'Let u = (x - 100)/10 and v = (y - 200)/20. If r_uv is computed as 0.70, what is the correlation coefficient r_xy?', '0.70', '0.35', '0.07', '-0.70', 'a', 1, 'Recall the property: does correlation coefficient change under change of origin and positive change of scale?', 'Step-by-step working: Correlation coefficient is independent of change of origin and scale when scale factors h and k have the same sign (here h = 10 > 0, k = 20 > 0). Therefore, r_xy = r_uv = 0.70. Distractor analysis: Option B results from incorrectly dividing by 2; Option C results from dividing by h = 10; Option D results from introducing a negative sign erroneously.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0066', 5, 'If u = (x - 25)/5 and v = (y - 50)/(-5), and r_uv = 0.65, what is the value of r_xy?', '0.65', '-0.65', '0.13', '-0.13', 'b', 2, 'Check the algebraic signs of scale factors h and k.', 'Step-by-step working: Here h = 5 > 0 and k = -5 < 0. When scale factors have opposite algebraic signs, r_uv = -r_xy, which means r_xy = -r_uv = -0.65. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.65) results from forgetting that opposite signs invert the sign of r; Option C and D result from dividing by scale factors.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0067', 5, 'Given the pairs (X, Y): (102, 204), (104, 208), (106, 212), (108, 216). By defining u = x - 100 and v = y - 200, find the correlation coefficient r_xy.', '2.0', '0.5', '1.0', '-1.0', 'c', 2, 'Transform data to u and v, and observe their relationship.', 'Step-by-step working: u = [2, 4, 6, 8] and v = [4, 8, 12, 16]. Notice that v = 2u for all points. Since v is perfectly positively linearly related to u, r_uv = 1.0. Thus r_xy = 1.0. Distractor analysis: Option B (0.5) results from taking 1/slope; Option C (2.0) results from taking the slope v/u = 2 as r (violating |r| <= 1); Option D (-1.0) results from a sign confusion.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0068', 5, 'For n = 8 pairs, u = x - 15, v = y - 20, Σu = 0, Σv = 0, Σu² = 64, Σv² = 100, and Σuv = -80. What is r_xy?', '0.0', '1.0', '-0.8', '-1.0', 'd', 2, 'Calculate r_uv = Σuv / √(Σu² * Σv²).', 'Step-by-step working: r_uv = -80 / √(64 * 100) = -80 / √6400 = -80 / 80 = -1.0. Therefore, r_xy = -1.0. Distractor analysis: Option B (1.0) results from omitting the minus sign; Option C (-0.8) results from dividing -80 by 100; Option D (0.0) results from miscalculation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0069', 5, 'A student transforms variables using u = (x - 45)/2 and v = (y - 55)/2. If the correlation between u and v is 0.42, what is the correlation between x and y?', '0.42', '0.84', '0.21', '0.00', 'a', 1, 'Both scale factors are identical and positive (h = 2, k = 2).', 'Step-by-step working: Since h = 2 > 0 and k = 2 > 0 have the same sign, r_xy = r_uv = 0.42. Distractor analysis: Option B results from multiplying by 2; Option C results from dividing by 2; Option D results from incorrectly assuming scale transformations eliminate correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0070', 5, 'In an experiment, u = -(x - 10)/4 and v = -(y - 20)/5. If r_uv = 0.58, what is r_xy?', '-0.58', '0.58', '0.29', '-0.29', 'b', 2, 'Check if both h and k are negative.', 'Step-by-step working: Here h = -4 < 0 and k = -5 < 0. Since both h and k have the SAME algebraic sign (both negative), r_uv = r_xy. Therefore, r_xy = 0.58. Distractor analysis: Option B (-0.58) results from incorrectly thinking negative scale factors always make r negative; Option C and D result from dividing by scale factors.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0071', 5, 'A set of simultaneous observations recorded on two distinct variables X and Y for n units is defined as:', 'Univariate Data', 'Multivariate Matrix', 'Bivariate Data', 'Categorical Array', 'c', 1, 'Consider data involving exactly two variables.', 'Step-by-step working: As defined in the chapter, a set of observations made on two variables is called Bivariate Data, denoted by n ordered pairs (x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂), ..., (xₙ, yₙ). Distractor analysis: Option A refers to data on a single variable; Option C refers to three or more variables; Option D is a generic data structure term.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0072', 5, 'According to statistical theory, what is correlation?', 'A measure of central tendency of two variables', 'A measure of dispersion of a single variable', 'A method to find the arithmetic mean of two variables', 'A measure of association or mutual relationship between two variables', 'd', 1, 'Recall the textbook definition: "A correlation is a measure of..."', 'Step-by-step working: The chapter explicitly defines: "A correlation is a measure of association or relation" between two underlying variables in bivariate data. Distractor analysis: Option A refers to mean/median; Option C is arithmetic averaging; Option D refers to variance or standard deviation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0073', 5, 'Covariance between two variables X and Y is primarily a measure of:', 'Joint variation between the two variables', 'The ratio of the two means', 'The difference between individual standard deviations', 'Total frequency of observations', 'a', 1, 'Recall Section 5.2 definition of covariance.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.2 states: "Covariance is a measure of joint variation between the two variables." Distractor analysis: Option B describes a mean ratio; Option C describes dispersion difference; Option D describes sample size n.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0074', 5, 'If Cov(X, Y) = 24.5, what is the value of Cov(Y, X)?', '-24.5', '24.5', '1 / 24.5', '0', 'b', 1, 'Recall Property (i) of covariance regarding the order of variables.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (i) of covariance states that Cov(X, Y) = Cov(Y, X). Thus, Cov(Y, X) = 24.5. Distractor analysis: Option A introduces a false negative sign; Option C takes the reciprocal; Option D assumes order change cancels covariance.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0075', 5, 'If C is a constant real number, what is the value of Cov(X, C)?', 'Var(X)', 'C · Var(X)', '0', '1', 'c', 1, 'Recall Property (ii) of covariance.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (ii) of covariance states that Cov(X, C) = 0 where C is a constant, because a constant does not vary with X. Distractor analysis: Option A confuses constant with X itself; Option B incorrectly multiplies by C; Option D is a standard guess for multiplicative identity.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0076', 5, 'What is the relationship between Cov(X, X) and the variance of X?', 'Cov(X, X) = 0', 'Cov(X, X) = σx', 'Cov(X, X) = 1', 'Cov(X, X) = Var(X)', 'd', 1, 'Recall Property (iv) of covariance.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (iv) states: Cov(X, X) = (1/n) Σ(x - x̄)² = Var(X). Distractor analysis: Option A confuses X with a constant; Option C confuses Cov(X,X) with correlation r(X,X); Option D confuses variance with standard deviation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0077', 5, 'If U = (X - a)/h and V = (Y - b)/k, where a, b, h, k are constants and h ≠ 0, k ≠ 0, which formula correctly relates Cov(U, V) and Cov(X, Y)?', 'Cov(U, V) = [1 / (hk)] · Cov(X, Y)', 'Cov(U, V) = hk · Cov(X, Y)', 'Cov(U, V) = Cov(X, Y) - ab', 'Cov(U, V) = (h + k) · Cov(X, Y)', 'a', 2, 'Recall Property (v) of covariance under change of scale.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (v) states: Covariance is invariant under change of origin but affected by scale: Cov(U, V) = [1 / (hk)] Cov(X, Y), which equivalently gives Cov(X, Y) = hk · Cov(U, V). Distractor analysis: Option A inverts the factor hk; Option C incorrectly subtracts origin constants; Option D uses addition instead of product.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0078', 5, 'If Cov(X, Y) = 36, and new variables are defined as U = (X - 10)/2 and V = (Y - 15)/3, find Cov(U, V).', '216', '6', '36', '18', 'b', 2, 'Apply Cov(U, V) = [1 / (hk)] Cov(X, Y).', 'Step-by-step working: Here h = 2 and k = 3. Using Cov(U, V) = [1 / (h * k)] Cov(X, Y), we get Cov(U, V) = 36 / (2 * 3) = 36 / 6 = 6. Distractor analysis: Option B (216) results from multiplying by hk = 6 instead of dividing; Option C (36) assumes covariance is completely invariant to scale; Option D (18) results from dividing by 2 only.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0079', 5, 'Karl Pearson correlation coefficient r_xy between two variables X and Y is mathematically defined as:', 'r_xy = (σx · σy) / Cov(X, Y)', 'r_xy = Cov(X, Y) / (σx² · σy²)', 'r_xy = Cov(X, Y) / (σx · σy)', 'r_xy = Cov(X, Y) / (σx + σy)', 'c', 1, 'Recall Section 5.4 definition of correlation coefficient.', 'Step-by-step working: The Karl Pearson correlation coefficient is defined as r_xy = Cov(X, Y) / (σx · σy). Distractor analysis: Option B uses variances instead of standard deviations; Option C inverts numerator and denominator; Option D uses the sum of standard deviations.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0080', 5, 'Which of the following is a fundamental property of Karl Pearson correlation coefficient r_xy?', 'r_xy = -r_yx', 'r_xy + r_yx = 0', 'r_xy = 1 / r_yx', 'r_xy = r_yx (order of variables is not important)', 'd', 1, 'Does the order in which variables are written affect correlation?', 'Step-by-step working: Property (i) of correlation coefficient states: r_xy = r_yx (the order of variables is not important). Distractor analysis: Option A incorrectly asserts anti-symmetry; Option C posits reciprocal relation; Option D claims mutual cancellation.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0081', 5, 'What is the correlation coefficient of any random variable X with itself, i.e., r(X, X)?', '1', '0', 'Var(X)', 'Infinity', 'a', 1, 'Recall Property (iv) of correlation coefficient.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (iv) states: r(X, X) = Cov(X, X) / (σx · σx) = Var(X) / Var(X) = 1. Distractor analysis: Option A confuses correlation with covariance of independent variables; Option C confuses r(X,X) with Cov(X,X); Option D is mathematically invalid.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0082', 5, 'The numerical value of Karl Pearson correlation coefficient r always satisfies which inequality?', '0 ≤ r ≤ 1', '-1 ≤ r ≤ 1', '-∞ < r < ∞', '-1 < r < 1 (strict inequality only)', 'b', 1, 'Recall Property (v) of correlation coefficient.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (v) states that r lies between -1 and 1 inclusive, i.e., -1 ≤ r ≤ 1. Distractor analysis: Option A ignores negative correlation; Option C confuses r with unbounded regression coefficients; Option D wrongly excludes the boundary values -1 and 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0083', 5, 'If the correlation coefficient between X and Y is 0.8, what is the correlation coefficient between (X - 5)/7 and (Y - 3)/8?', '0.08', '0.4', '0.8', '-0.8', 'c', 2, 'Recall Property (ii) regarding change of origin and scale with positive scale factors.', 'Step-by-step working: The correlation coefficient does not change its magnitude under change of origin and scale when scale factors have the same sign. Here h = 7 > 0 and k = 8 > 0, so r_uv = r_xy = 0.8. Distractor analysis: Option B results from dividing by 2; Option C results from dividing by 10; Option D introduces an unwarranted negative sign.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0084', 5, 'If the correlation coefficient between X and Y is 0.65, what is the correlation coefficient between (X - 4)/3 and (Y - 8)/(-5)?', '0.65', '0.00', '-0.13', '-0.65', 'd', 2, 'Note the signs of h = 3 and k = -5.', 'Step-by-step working: Property (iii) states: If h and k have opposite algebraic signs, r_uv = -r_xy. Here h = 3 > 0 and k = -5 < 0, so r_uv = -0.65. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.65) ignores the negative sign of k; Option C (-0.13) divides by 5; Option D (0.00) assumes opposite scales cancel the relationship.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0085', 5, 'If r(X, Y) = -0.45, what is r(-2X + 3, -4Y - 5)?', '-0.45', '0.45', '-0.90', '0.00', 'a', 2, 'Check the signs of the multipliers of X and Y.', 'Step-by-step working: Let U = -2X + 3 and V = -4Y - 5. The scale factors are h = -2 and k = -4. Since both h and k are negative, they have the SAME algebraic sign. Therefore, r_uv = r_xy = -0.45. Distractor analysis: Option A (0.45) incorrectly changes the sign of r because two negatives make a positive in multiplication; Option C (-0.90) doubles the value; Option D (0.00) is arbitrary.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0086', 5, 'If height is measured in inches and weight in pounds by one investigator, while another measures height in cm and weight in kg for the same subjects, what will be observed about their calculated correlation coefficients?', 'The second investigator gets a much larger r due to metric units', 'Both investigators obtain exactly the same value of r', 'The second investigator gets a negative r', 'The values cannot be compared because r has units of kg/cm', 'b', 2, 'Remember that r is independent of scale and is a dimensionless pure number.', 'Step-by-step working: Since converting inches to cm and pounds to kg involves positive linear scale transformations (change of scale with positive constants), and because correlation coefficient is invariant under change of scale, both investigators get the exact same value of r. Furthermore, r is a pure dimensionless number. Distractor analysis: Option A mistakenly assumes larger numerical data yields larger r; Option C assumes unit conversion flips the direction; Option D falsely claims r has units.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0087', 5, 'According to the standard interpretation guidelines, if r = 0.85, the correlation between the two variables is classified as:', 'Moderate positive correlation', 'Perfect positive correlation', 'High positive correlation', 'Insignificant correlation', 'c', 1, 'Recall Section 5.6: If r > 0.8, what is the category?', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.6 states: "If r > 0.8, there is high positive correlation." Distractor analysis: Option A is for 0.3 < r < 0.8; Option C is strictly for r = 1; Option D is for |r| < 0.3.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0088', 5, 'If the calculated correlation coefficient is r = 0.55, how is it interpreted according to the text?', 'High positive correlation', 'Perfect positive correlation', 'Poor or insignificant correlation', 'Moderate positive correlation', 'd', 1, 'Recall the range 0.3 < r < 0.8.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.6 states: "If 0.3 < r < 0.8, there is moderate positive correlation." Thus, r = 0.55 is moderate positive. Distractor analysis: Option A requires r > 0.8; Option C requires |r| < 0.3; Option D requires r = 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0089', 5, 'When the correlation coefficient satisfies |r| < 0.3, the correlation between the two variables is described as:', 'Insignificant or poor', 'Moderate', 'High negative', 'Perfect', 'a', 1, 'Recall the textbook interpretation for |r| < 0.3.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.6 states: "If |r| < 0.3, the correlation is insignificant or poor." Distractor analysis: Option A requires 0.3 < r < 0.8; Option C requires r < -0.8; Option D requires |r| = 1.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0090', 5, 'In a scatter diagram, if all the plotted points fall exactly along a straight line sloping downward from left to right, it indicates:', 'Perfect positive correlation (r = 1)', 'Perfect negative correlation (r = -1)', 'Negative correlation with low degree', 'Zero correlation (r = 0)', 'b', 1, 'Look at the direction of the line: falling from left to right.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.5 states: "If the points are falling from left to right in a straight line then scatter diagram indicates a perfect negative correlation" with r = -1. Distractor analysis: Option A is for rising straight line; Option C is for a wide downward band (not a line); Option D is for no linear trend.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0091', 5, 'Which of the following scatter diagram patterns indicates zero correlation (r = 0)?', 'Points clustered tightly along an upward straight line', 'Points clustered tightly along a downward straight line', 'Points lying along a horizontal straight line parallel to the X-axis', 'A narrow band of points rising from left to right', 'c', 1, 'Review Fig 5.9 and 5.11 showing zero correlation.', 'Step-by-step working: In a scatter diagram, when points lie on a horizontal line parallel to the X-axis (or vertical line parallel to the Y-axis, or form a completely dispersed cloud with no trend), there is no variation in one variable relative to the other, so r = 0. Distractor analysis: Option A is perfect positive (r = 1); Option C is perfect negative (r = -1); Option D is positive correlation of high degree.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0092', 5, 'When points on a scatter diagram form a downward-sloping band from left to right, and the width of the band is relatively large (bigger), what is the correct interpretation?', 'Negative correlation with high degree', 'Perfect negative correlation', 'Positive correlation with low degree', 'Negative correlation with low degree', 'd', 1, 'Downward band means negative; bigger width means lower degree.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.5 (II)(c) states: "If the band is falling down from left to right it indicates negative correlation. If the width of the band is bigger then the correlation is of low degree." Distractor analysis: Option A describes a narrow band falling down; Option C has the wrong sign (rising band); Option D requires all points to lie on a single straight line.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0093', 5, 'When arithmetic means x̄ and ȳ are small integers, which formula is most conveniently used to compute r directly from deviations?', 'r = Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) / [ √Σ(x - x̄)² · √Σ(y - ȳ)² ]', 'r = [ nΣxy - ΣxΣy ] / [ nΣx² - (Σx)² ]', 'r = Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) / n²', 'r = [ ΣxΣy ] / [ √Σx² · √Σy² ]', 'a', 1, 'Look at Alternative formula (i) in Section 5.6.', 'Step-by-step working: Alternative formula (i) states: r = Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) / [ √Σ(x - x̄)² · √Σ(y - ȳ)² ], which is used when x̄ and ȳ are integers. Distractor analysis: Option B lacks the square root and y-term in the denominator; Option C divides by n²; Option D uses uncentered raw sums incorrectly.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0094', 5, 'Which of the following expressions correctly gives the standard deviation σx as presented in the chapter?', 'σx = (1/n) Σx² - (x̄)²', 'σx = √[ (1/n) Σx² - (x̄)² ]', 'σx = √[ (1/n) Σx - x̄ ]', 'σx = [ Σ(x - x̄)² ] / n', 'b', 1, 'Look at the formula for σx in Section 5.3.', 'Step-by-step working: Section 5.3 defines: σx = √[ (1/n) Σ(x - x̄)² ] = √[ (1/n) Σx² - (x̄)² ]. Distractor analysis: Option B gives variance, not standard deviation (missing square root); Option C has incorrect terms inside square root; Option D gives variance without the square root.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0095', 5, 'The correlation coefficient between X and Y is 0.3, their covariance is 12, and the variance of X is 16. What is the standard deviation of Y?', '25', '4', '10', '40', 'c', 2, 'Find σx = √Var(X) first, then rearrange r = cov(x, y) / (σx · σy).', 'Step-by-step working: Given Var(X) = 16 => σx = √16 = 4. cov(x, y) = 12, r = 0.3. Since r = cov(x, y) / (σx · σy), we have 0.3 = 12 / (4 · σy) = 3 / σy. Therefore, σy = 3 / 0.3 = 10. Distractor analysis: Option B (4) is σx, not σy; Option C (25) results from squaring an intermediate step; Option D (40) results from failing to divide by σx (12 / 0.3 = 40).'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0096', 5, 'The correlation coefficient between two variables X and Y is 0.48, their covariance is 36, and the variance of X is 16. What is the standard deviation of Y?', '75.00', '9.00', '4.00', '18.75', 'd', 2, 'Use σx = √16 = 4 and solve 0.48 = 36 / (4 · σy).', 'Step-by-step working: σx = √16 = 4. r = cov(x, y) / (σx · σy) => 0.48 = 36 / (4 · σy) = 9 / σy. Thus σy = 9 / 0.48 = 18.75. Distractor analysis: Option B (9.00) results from forgetting to divide by r = 0.48; Option C (4.00) is σx; Option D (75.00) results from omitting σx from the denominator (36 / 0.48 = 75).'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0097', 5, 'Given r = 0.15, σy = 4, Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 12, and Σ(x - x̄)² = 40. What is the number of pairs of observations n?', '10', '20', '15', '25', 'a', 3, 'Express cov(x,y) and σx in terms of n, then solve r = cov(x,y) / (σx · σy).', 'Step-by-step working: cov(x, y) = 12 / n. σx = √(40 / n). σy = 4. r = (12 / n) / [ √(40/n) · 4 ] = 3 / [ √n · √40 ] = 3 / √(40n). Given r = 0.15: 0.15 = 3 / √(40n) => √(40n) = 3 / 0.15 = 20. Squaring both sides gives 40n = 400 => n = 10. Distractor analysis: Option B (20) is the value of √(40n) before squaring and dividing; Option C (15) results from using r = 0.20; Option D (25) results from an algebra slip.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0098', 5, 'Given r = 0.4, σy = 3, Σ(x - x̄)(y - ȳ) = 108, and Σ(x - x̄)² = 900. Find the number of pairs of observations n.', '6', '9', '12', '16', 'b', 3, 'Express σx as 30/√n and cov as 108/n, then equate to r.', 'Step-by-step working: σx = √(900 / n) = 30 / √n. cov(x, y) = 108 / n. r = cov / (σx · σy) = (108 / n) / [ (30 / √n) · 3 ] = (108 / n) / (90 / √n) = 1.2 / √n. Given r = 0.4: 0.4 = 1.2 / √n => √n = 1.2 / 0.4 = 3 => n = 3² = 9. Distractor analysis: Option B (6) results from taking 2 * 3; Option C (12) results from dividing 108/9; Option D (16) results from miscalculating √n = 4.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0099', 5, 'In a bivariate data set of 5 observations, the arithmetic mean of Y is ȳ = 8. Four of the Y values are 9, 11, 8, and 7, while one value is missing. What is the missing value?', '6', '8', '5', '10', 'c', 1, 'Recall that Σy = n · ȳ.', 'Step-by-step working: Total sum of Y values is Σy = n · ȳ = 5 · 8 = 40. The sum of the four known observations is 9 + 11 + 8 + 7 = 35. Therefore, the missing observation is 40 - 35 = 5. Distractor analysis: Option B (8) is simply the mean ȳ; Option C (6) is the mean of X; Option D (10) results from subtracting 35 from 45.'),
        ('mth11b-05-mcq-0100', 5, 'If the correlation coefficient between X and Y is 0.72, what is the correlation coefficient between X + 15 and Y - 25?', '0.00', '-0.72', '0.62', '0.72', 'd', 1, 'Recall the effect of adding or subtracting constants (change of origin) on r.', 'Step-by-step working: Karl Pearson correlation coefficient is completely invariant under change of origin. Adding 15 to X and subtracting 25 from Y are shifts of origin (with scale factors h = 1, k = 1). Hence, the correlation coefficient remains exactly 0.72. Distractor analysis: Option B introduces an unwarranted negative sign from the subtraction in Y; Option C subtracts (25 - 15)/100; Option D assumes origin shifts destroy the correlation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0001', 6, 'A library has 4 fiction titles and 6 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '11', '2', '10', '24', 'c', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '4 + 6 = 10. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0002', 6, 'A library has 7 fiction titles and 5 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '35', '13', '2', '12', 'd', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '7 + 5 = 12. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0003', 6, 'A library has 9 fiction titles and 8 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '72', '1', '17', '18', 'c', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '9 + 8 = 17. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0004', 6, 'A library has 12 fiction titles and 7 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '84', '5', '20', '19', 'd', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '12 + 7 = 19. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0005', 6, 'A library has 15 fiction titles and 9 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '135', '25', '6', '24', 'd', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '15 + 9 = 24. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0006', 6, 'A library has 6 fiction titles and 11 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '5', '66', '17', '18', 'c', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '6 + 11 = 17. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0007', 6, 'A library has 13 fiction titles and 14 non-fiction titles. If one title is selected, how many choices are possible?', '182', '28', '27', '1', 'c', 1, 'Add the counts because only one category is selected.', '13 + 14 = 27. Option A results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option B results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
