        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0229', 6, 'Find n if 10P5 = 42 × nP3.', '10', '9', '45', '7', 'a', 2, 'Cancel the common nPr factors, then solve the resulting product of two consecutive integers.', '10P5/10P3 = (7)(6) = 7×6 = 42; hence n−r=7 and n=10. Option B results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0230', 6, 'Find n if 12P6 = 56 × nP4.', '12', '11', '60', '8', 'a', 2, 'Cancel the common nPr factors, then solve the resulting product of two consecutive integers.', '12P6/12P4 = (8)(7) = 8×7 = 56; hence n−r=8 and n=12. Option B results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0231', 6, 'How many distinct arrangements can be formed from all letters of BALLOON?', '1261', '2520', '1260', '5040', 'c', 1, 'Use n! divided by the factorial of each repeated-letter count.', '7! divided by 4 gives 1260. Option A results from treating repeated objects as distinct. Option B results from correcting for only one repeated group. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the repetition correction.'),
