        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0351', 6, 'In which situation is the addition principle appropriate?', 'When alternatives have no common outcomes', 'When repetition is required', 'When two operations must both be completed', 'When order must be ignored', 'a', 1, 'Check whether the outcomes are disjoint alternatives.', 'The addition principle applies to mutually exclusive alternatives; their counts are added. Option B results from adding and multiplying the two counts instead. Option C results from subtracting the two alternatives. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after choosing the correct operation.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0352', 6, 'If one operation can be done in m ways and a second successive operation in n ways, how many outcomes result when both are performed?', 'm−n', 'm/n', 'm+n', 'mn', 'd', 1, 'Both operations are completed successively, so multiply.', 'By the multiplication principle, the total is m×n=mn. Option A results from adding the independent choices instead of multiplying. Option B results from omitting one factor from the product. Option C results from using an extra factor that is not part of the stated choices.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0353', 6, 'What does a complete tree diagram represent?', 'Only outcomes involving multiplication', 'Only repeated outcomes', 'All possible divisions or outcomes of the process', 'Only the most likely outcome', 'c', 1, 'Think of each branch as a possible outcome.', 'A complete tree diagram displays the possible branches/outcomes of the process. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option B results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0354', 6, 'The collection of all possible outcomes of an experiment is called its:', 'combination', 'factorial', 'sample space', 'permutation', 'c', 1, 'Use the probability/counting terminology for all possible outcomes.', 'The sample space is the collection of all possible outcomes. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option B results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0355', 6, 'Which principle says that changing the order in which independent counting activities are considered does not change the total count?', 'Factorial principle', 'Combination principle', 'Addition principle', 'Invariance principle of counting', 'd', 1, 'Focus on the effect of changing the order of counting steps.', 'The invariance principle states that the count is unchanged when the order of the counting activities is changed. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option B results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option C results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0356', 6, 'What is 0! according to the factorial definition used here?', '1', 'undefined', '0', '−1', 'a', 1, 'Recall the special factorial value at zero.', 'By definition, 0! = 1. Option B results from using a difference/quotient of factorials instead of the stated factorial. Option C results from stopping the factorial product one factor early. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after evaluating the factorial.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0357', 6, 'For a positive integer n, which identity is correct?', 'n! = n(n−1)!', 'n! = n+(n−1)!', 'n! = (n−1)!/n', 'n! = n!−1', 'a', 1, 'Separate the first factor n from the remaining product.', 'n! = n×(n−1)! by the factorial product definition. Option B results from using a difference/quotient of factorials instead of the stated factorial. Option C results from stopping the factorial product one factor early. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after evaluating the factorial.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0358', 6, 'For natural numbers m and n, which statement is generally false?', '(m+n)! is divisible by m! and n!', 'n! = n(n−1)!', '0! = 1', '(m+n)! = m! + n!', 'd', 1, 'Factorials do not distribute over addition.', 'The chapter notes that (m+n)! is not generally equal to m!+n!. Option A results from using a difference/quotient of factorials instead of the stated factorial. Option B results from stopping the factorial product one factor early. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after evaluating the factorial.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0359', 6, 'For natural numbers m and n, which equality is not generally valid when m is divisible by n?', 'n! = n(n−1)!', '(m+n)! is divisible by m! and n!', '(m÷n)! = m!÷n!', '0! = 1', 'c', 1, 'Factorial does not distribute over division.', 'The chapter notes that (m÷n)! is not generally m!÷n!. Option A results from using a difference/quotient of factorials instead of the stated factorial. Option B results from stopping the factorial product one factor early. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after evaluating the factorial.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0360', 6, 'A permutation is best described as:', 'A sample space', 'An ordered arrangement of some or all objects', 'A factorial value', 'An unordered selection only', 'b', 1, 'Order is the key feature.', 'A permutation is an ordered arrangement of some or all objects. Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0361', 6, 'Which formula gives the number of permutations of n distinct objects taken r at a time without repetition?', 'n!/(n−r)!', 'n!/[r!(n−r)!]', 'n^r', '(n−r)!/n!', 'a', 1, 'Permutations retain order, so there is no r! divisor.', 'The formula is nPr = n!/(n−r)!. Option B results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0362', 6, 'What is nP0?', '0', 'n', '1', 'n!', 'c', 1, 'A zero-length arrangement has one empty arrangement.', 'The property gives nP0=1. Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option B results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0363', 6, 'What is nP1?', 'n!', '1', 'n−1', 'n', 'd', 1, 'One position can be filled by any of the n objects.', 'Thus nP1=n. Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option B results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option C results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0364', 6, 'Which relation is correct for permutations?', 'nPr = r(n−1)Pr', 'nPr = n+r', 'nPr = n(n−1)P(r−1)', 'nPr = n/r', 'c', 1, 'Separate the first choice from the remaining r−1 positions.', 'After choosing the first object in n ways, the rest are arranged as (n−1)P(r−1). Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option B results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0365', 6, 'For n distinct objects taken r at a time, which condition is required for the standard nPr formula?', 'r must be zero', 'r ≤ n', 'r = n+1', 'r > n', 'b', 1, 'You cannot select more distinct objects than are available.', 'The formula is stated for r≤n. Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0366', 6, 'When repetitions are allowed, the number of permutations of n distinct objects taken r at a time is:', 'n^r', 'n!', 'nPr', 'n+r', 'a', 1, 'Each of the r positions has n choices again.', 'There are n choices independently for each of r positions, giving n^r. Option B results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option C results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0367', 6, 'For n distinct objects taken all at a time, when two specified objects must always be together, which count applies?', '(n−2)(n−1)!', '2(n−1)!', 'n!', '2n!', 'b', 2, 'Treat the two specified objects as one block, then account for their internal order.', 'The block plus the other n−2 objects gives n−1 units: (n−1)! arrangements, and the pair has 2 orders. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option C results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0368', 6, 'For n distinct objects taken all at a time, when two specified objects are never together, which count applies?', '2(n−1)!', '(n−2)(n−1)!', 'n!', '(n−2)!', 'b', 2, 'Subtract the together cases from all arrangements.', 'Total n! minus together 2(n−1)! equals (n−2)(n−1)!. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option C results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0369', 6, 'For n distinct objects taken r at a time, with a specified object always included, the count is:', 'r(n−1)P(r−1)', '(n−1)Pr', 'nPr', 'r nPr', 'a', 2, 'Choose the position of the specified object, then fill the remaining positions.', 'The specified object has r possible positions and the other r−1 positions are filled from n−1 objects: r(n−1)P(r−1). Option B results from using an incorrect operation. Option C results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0370', 6, 'For n distinct objects taken r at a time, with a specified object excluded, the count is:', '(n−r)Pr', 'r(n−1)P(r−1)', '(n−1)Pr', 'nPr', 'c', 1, 'Remove the specified object from the available pool.', 'The remaining n−1 objects are arranged r at a time: (n−1)Pr. Option A results from using an incorrect operation. Option B results from using a related but incorrect formula. Option D results from an arithmetic slip.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0371', 6, 'If n objects contain repeated groups of sizes n1,n2,...,nk, what denominator is used in the all-at-once permutation count?', 'n1! n2! ... nk!', '(n1+n2+...+nk)!', 'n!', 'n1+n2+...+nk', 'a', 2, 'Divide by a factorial for each indistinguishable group.', 'The count is n!/(n1!n2!...nk!), so the denominator is the product of repeated-group factorials. Option B results from treating repeated objects as distinct. Option C results from correcting for only one repeated group. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the repetition correction.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0372', 6, 'How many circular arrangements of n distinct objects are there when rotations are considered the same?', 'n!/n!', '(n−1)!', 'n', 'n!', 'b', 1, 'Fix one object and arrange the remaining objects.', 'Fixing one object leaves n−1 objects to arrange, giving (n−1)!. Option A results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option C results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option D results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0373', 6, 'If clockwise and anticlockwise mirror arrangements are not considered different, the circular count for n distinct objects is:', '(n−1)!/2', '(n−1)!', '2(n−1)!', 'n!', 'a', 1, 'First use the circular count, then identify mirror pairs.', 'Each arrangement and its mirror are treated as one, so divide (n−1)! by 2. Option B results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option C results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option D results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0374', 6, 'For circular arrangements of n distinct objects taken r at a time, when clockwise and anticlockwise arrangements are considered different, the count is:', '(n−1)!', 'nPr', 'nPr/(2r)', 'nPr/r', 'd', 2, 'Use the stated circular-permutation formula for r selected objects.', 'The stated formula is nPr divided by r. Option A results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option B results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option C results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0375', 6, 'For circular arrangements of n distinct objects taken r at a time, when clockwise and anticlockwise arrangements are not considered different, the count is:', '2nPr', '(n−1)!', 'nPr/(2r)', 'nPr/r', 'c', 2, 'Start with the circular count and then identify mirror pairs.', 'The stated formula is nPr/(2r). Option A results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option B results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option D results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0376', 6, 'A combination differs from a permutation because in a combination:', 'The order always matters', 'All objects must be selected', 'Repetition is mandatory', 'The order of selected objects does not matter', 'd', 1, 'Focus on whether rearranging selected objects creates a new selection.', 'In a combination, selection order is immaterial. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0377', 6, 'Which formula gives nCr for distinct objects without repetition?', 'n^r', 'n!/(n−r)!', 'r!(n−r)!/n!', 'n!/[r!(n−r)!]', 'd', 1, 'Combinations divide permutations by r!.', 'nCr = n!/[r!(n−r)!]. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0378', 6, 'Which identity connects permutations and combinations?', 'nCr = nPr × r!', 'nCr/r! = nPr', 'nCr × r! = nPr', 'nCr + r! = nPr', 'c', 1, 'A selected r-set can be arranged in r! orders.', 'Each r-element combination gives r! permutations, so nCr×r!=nPr. Option A results from using combinations, which ignore order. Option B results from allowing repetition when it is forbidden. Option D results from using an incomplete permutation product.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0379', 6, 'For 0≤r≤n, which symmetry property holds?', 'nCr = nC(r+1)', 'nCr = n+r', 'nCr = rCn', 'nCr = nC(n−r)', 'd', 1, 'Selecting r objects is equivalent to selecting the n−r objects left out.', 'The chapter gives nCr=nC(n−r). Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0380', 6, 'What is nC0?', 'n', 'n!', '1', '0', 'c', 1, 'There is exactly one way to select nothing.', 'nC0=1. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0381', 6, 'What is nCn?', 'n!', '1', '0', 'n', 'b', 1, 'There is exactly one way to select all n objects.', 'nCn=1. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option C results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0382', 6, 'If nCr = nCs, which alternatives are possible?', 's=r+1 only', 's=r−n only', 's=r or s=n−r', 's=n+r only', 'c', 1, 'Use the symmetry of the binomial coefficients.', 'Equality occurs when the lower indices are equal or complementary. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0383', 6, 'Which Pascal-type identity is correct?', 'nCr + nC(r+1) = nCr', 'nCr − nC(r−1) = (n+1)Cr', 'nCr + nC(r−1) = (n+1)Cr', 'nCr × nC(r−1) = (n+1)Cr', 'c', 1, 'Combine adjacent coefficients with the same upper index.', 'The stated identity is nCr+nC(r−1)=(n+1)Cr. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0384', 6, 'What is the value of nC0+nC1+...+nCn?', 'n!', 'n^2', '2n', '2^n', 'd', 1, 'Use the complete sum property for all r.', 'The stated identity gives the sum as 2^n. Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option C results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0385', 6, 'How do the sums of even-indexed and odd-indexed binomial coefficients compare?', 'The odd sum is zero', 'They are both 2^n', 'They are both 2^(n−1)', 'The even sum is n!', 'c', 2, 'Use the parity-splitting property.', 'The chapter states each parity sum equals 2^(n−1). Option A results from using permutations, which count order. Option B results from using the wrong complementary or adjacent index. Option D results from an arithmetic slip after applying the combination identity.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0387', 6, 'How many circular arrangements are possible for 8 objects when 3 of the objects are identical and the remaining objects are distinct?', '1680', '840', '5040', '6720', 'b', 2, 'Use (n−1)! and divide by the factorial of the indistinguishable group.', 'Required count = (8−1)!/3! = 840. Option A results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option C results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option D results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0389', 6, 'How many circular arrangements are possible for 10 objects when 4 of the objects are identical and the remaining objects are distinct?', '15120', '90720', '362880', '151200', 'a', 2, 'Use (n−1)! and divide by the factorial of the indistinguishable group.', 'Required count = (10−1)!/4! = 15120. Option B results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option C results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option D results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-06-mcq-0390', 6, 'How many circular arrangements are possible for 11 objects when 3 of the objects are identical and the remaining objects are distinct?', '6652800', '3628800', '1209600', '604800', 'd', 2, 'Use (n−1)! and divide by the factorial of the indistinguishable group.', 'Required count = (11−1)!/3! = 604800. Option A results from using the linear n! count instead of removing rotations. Option B results from forgetting the internal order of a required block. Option C results from failing to identify mirror images when required.'),
        ('mth11b-07-mcq-0001', 7, 'The experiment consists of tossing a coin and a standard die. Which set correctly represents its sample space?', '{(1,H), (2,H), (3,H), (4,H), (5,H), (6,H), (1,T), (2,T), (3,T), (4,T), (5,T), (6,T)}', '{(H,1), (H,2), (H,3), (H,4), (H,5), (H,6)}', '{(H,1), (H,2), (H,3), (H,4), (H,5), (H,6), (T,1), (T,2), (T,3), (T,4), (T,5), (T,6)}', '{(H,1), (H,2), (H,3), (H,4), (H,5), (H,6), (T,1), (T,2), (T,3), (T,4), (T,5)}', 'c', 1, 'List every possible coin result with every possible die result.', '1. There are 2 coin outcomes and 6 die outcomes.
2. Pair each coin outcome with each die outcome, giving 2×6 ordered outcomes.

Wrong-option checks: Option A: reverses the ordered positions of the coin and die; Option B: omits every outcome with T; Option D: omits one tail outcome'),
        ('mth11b-07-mcq-0002', 7, 'The experiment consists of tossing a coin and a four-sided die labeled 1–4. Which set correctly represents its sample space?', '{(H,1), (T,1)}', '{(1,H), (1,T)}', '14/13', '{(H,1)}', 'a', 1, 'List every possible coin result with every possible die result.', '1. There are 2 coin outcomes and 1 die outcomes.
