Option C: reporting amount instead of interest..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0334', 9, 'A principal of Rs. 7200 earns simple interest at 5% p.a. for 3 years. What is the amount?', 'Rs. 1080', 'Rs. 828', 'Rs. 8280', 'Rs. 9360', 'c', 1, 'Find simple interest and add it to principal.', 'I=7200×3×5/100=1080; A=7200+1080=8280.
Option A: reporting interest.
Option B: dividing interest by 10.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0335', 9, 'For compound interest calculated half-yearly at an annual rate of 8%, what rate is used for each half-year?', '8%', '4%', '16%', '2%', 'b', 1, 'Halve the annual rate.', '8%/2 = 4%.
Option A: using the annual rate unchanged.
Option C: halving twice..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0336', 9, 'For quarterly compounding at an annual rate of 12%, what rate applies per quarter?', '3%', '6%', '12%', '48%', 'a', 1, 'Divide the annual rate by four.', '12/4 = 3%.
Option B: leaving the annual rate unchanged.
Option C: multiplying by four..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0337', 9, 'If compound interest is calculated half-yearly for 3 years, how many compounding periods are there?', '3', '5', '12', '6', 'd', 1, 'There are two half-years per year.', '3×2 = 6.
Option A: using years as periods.
Option B: subtracting one period.
Option C: using four periods per year..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0338', 9, 'If compound interest is calculated quarterly for 2.5 years, how many compounding periods occur?', '5', '8', '10', '12', 'c', 1, 'There are four quarters per year.', '2.5×4 = 10.
Option A: using two periods per year.
Option B: using only whole years.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0339', 9, 'The difference between compound interest and simple interest for 2 years at 10% on principal P is what expression?', '2P/100', 'P/100', '10P/100', 'P/10', 'b', 2, 'Use the two-year difference formula Pr²/100².', 'P×10²/100² = P/100.
Option A: using Pr/100.
Option C: simplifying 10/100 incorrectly..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0340', 9, 'If rates are r1%, r2%, and r3% for three successive years, which amount factor is correct?', '(1+r1/100)(1+r2/100)(1+r3/100)', '1+(r1+r2+r3)/100', '(1+r1+r2+r3)/100', '(1+r1/100)+(1+r2/100)+(1+r3/100)', 'a', 3, 'Multiply the annual growth factors successively.', 'Each year''s amount becomes the principal for the next year, so the three factors are multiplied.
Option B: dividing the entire factor by 100.
Option C: adding factors instead of multiplying..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0341', 9, 'An asset worth Rs. 80,000 depreciates by 10% in one year. What is its value after one year?', 'Rs. 8,000', 'Rs. 88,000', 'Rs. 70,000', 'Rs. 72,000', 'd', 1, 'Retain 90% of the value.', '80000×90/100=72000.
Option A: giving only depreciation amount.
Option B: increasing by 10%.
Option C: subtracting 10000..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0342', 9, 'An asset''s value after one year at 20% depreciation is Rs. 64,000. What was its initial value?', 'Rs. 51,200', 'Rs. 76,800', 'Rs. 80,000', 'Rs. 84,000', 'c', 1, 'Divide the later value by 0.80.', '64000/0.8=80000.
Option A: applying another 20% reduction.
Option B: multiplying by 0.8.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0343', 9, 'A and B invest Rs. 30,000 and Rs. 45,000 for the same period. In what ratio should profit be divided?', '3:2', '2:3', '30:45', '1:3', 'b', 1, 'For equal time, profit ratio equals investment ratio.', '30000:45000 simplifies to 2:3.
Option A: reversing the ratio.
Option C: dividing 45,000 by 30,000 incorrectly..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0344', 9, 'A and B invest equal amounts, but A keeps the money for 8 months and B for 12 months. What is their profit ratio?', '2:3', '3:2', '8:12', '1:2', 'a', 1, 'For equal capital, use the time ratio.', '8:12 = 2:3.
Option B: treating months as years without changing both.
Option C: reducing 8:12 to 1:2..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0345', 9, 'A invests Rs. 20,000 for 6 months and B invests Rs. 30,000 for 4 months. What is the profit ratio?', '2:3', '3:2', '5:1', '1:1', 'd', 1, 'Use capital × time.', 'A: 20000×6=120000; B:30000×4=120000, so 1:1.
Option A: using capital only.
Option B: using time only.
Option C: adding capital and time..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0346', 9, 'If GST is 18% and it is divided equally between CGST and SGST, what is each component rate?', '18%', '36%', '9%', '4.5%', 'c', 1, 'Divide the total GST rate by two.', '18/2=9%.
Option A: charging full GST as each component.
Option B: doubling GST.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0347', 9, 'A trader has output tax Rs. 4200 and input tax credit Rs. 3100. What GST is payable?', 'Rs. 7300', 'Rs. 1100', 'Rs. 3100', 'Rs. 1400', 'b', 1, 'GST payable = output tax − ITC.', '4200−3100=1100.
Option A: adding output and ITC.
Option C: subtracting output from ITC..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0348', 9, 'For aggregate turnover above Rs. 1.5 crore and below Rs. 5 crore, what HSN code length applies?', '2 digits', '4 digits', '8 digits', '1 digit', 'a', 2, 'Use the stated turnover band and its corresponding HSN structure.', 'The stated band uses a 2-digit HSN code.
Option B: using importer/exporter coding.
Option C: choosing an arbitrary digit count..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0349', 9, 'For aggregate turnover above Rs. 5 crore, what HSN code length applies?', '2 digits', '8 digits', '6 digits', '4 digits', 'd', 1, 'Match the turnover band above Rs. 5 crore.', 'The stated structure specifies 4 digits.
Option A: using the lower turnover band.
Option B: using importer/exporter coding.
Option C: using an unstated six-digit rule..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0350', 9, 'For an importer or exporter, what HSN code length applies?', '2 digits', '4 digits', '8 digits', '10 digits', 'c', 1, 'Use the importer/exporter rule stated for HSN.', 'The stated importer/exporter structure uses 8 digits.
Option A: using the lower turnover structure.
Option B: using the above-Rs.5-crore structure.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0351', 9, 'A business has aggregate turnover above Rs. 20 lakh in a financial year. What turnover threshold is used for GST liability, subject to the agriculture exception?', 'Rs. 10 lakh', 'Rs. 20 lakh', 'Rs. 50 lakh', 'Rs. 1 crore', 'b', 1, 'Recall the turnover threshold stated for businesses.', 'The stated threshold is above Rs. 20 lakh per financial year, with agriculture excepted under the stated rule.
Option A: halving the threshold.
Option C: multiplying the threshold by five..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0352', 9, 'A Rs. 100 share quoted at a premium of Rs. 16 has what market value?', 'Rs. 116', 'Rs. 84', 'Rs. 1600', 'Rs. 101.6', 'a', 1, 'At premium, add the premium to face value.', '100+16=116.
Option B: multiplying face value by premium.
Option C: treating 16 as a percentage rather than rupees..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0353', 9, 'A Rs. 100 share quoted at a discount of Rs. 7 has what market value?', 'Rs. 107', 'Rs. 700', 'Rs. 99.3', 'Rs. 93', 'd', 1, 'At discount, subtract the discount from face value.', '100−7=93.
Option A: adding the discount.
Option B: multiplying by 7.
Option C: treating 7 as a percentage..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0354', 9, 'A share costs Rs. 180 and brokerage is 0.5% on purchase. What is the brokerage amount?', 'Rs. 0.09', 'Rs. 9', 'Rs. 0.90', 'Rs. 180.50', 'c', 1, 'Brokerage = percentage of market value.', '180×0.5/100=0.90.
Option A: using 0.05%.
Option B: multiplying by 5%.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0355', 9, 'A share sells for Rs. 250 and brokerage is 0.4%. What amount is obtained after subtracting brokerage?', 'Rs. 250.40', 'Rs. 249', 'Rs. 249.60', 'Rs. 240', 'b', 1, 'On sale, subtract brokerage from selling price.', 'Brokerage=250×0.4/100=1; net=249.
Option A: adding brokerage on sale.
Option C: subtracting 10..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0356', 9, 'A Rs. 80 share pays 12% dividend. What is the annual dividend per share?', 'Rs. 9.60', 'Rs. 12', 'Rs. 6.67', 'Rs. 96', 'a', 1, 'Dividend is calculated on face value.', '80×12/100=9.60.
Option B: dividing face value by rate.
Option C: multiplying by 12 without dividing by 100..'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0357', 9, 'A partner invests capital and also manages the business. Which description applies to this partner?', '2 – Sleeping partner', '3 – Creditor', '4 – Debtor', '1 – Working partner', 'd', 1, 'Identify whether the partner manages the business as well as invests.', 'A partner who invests money and manages the business is a working partner.
Option A: confusing management with passive investment.
Option B: treating a lender as a partner.
Option C: treating a borrower as a partner.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0358', 9, 'A partner invests money but does not take part in the day-to-day affairs of the business. Which description applies?', '1 – Working partner', '3 – Creditor', '2 – Sleeping partner', '4 – Debtor', 'c', 1, 'Focus on whether the partner participates in daily management.', 'A partner who only invests and is not involved in day-to-day affairs is a sleeping partner.
Option A: assigning the management role to a non-managing partner.
Option B: confusing lending with partnership.
Option D: confusing borrowing with partnership.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0359', 9, 'Which GST component is levied by the Central Government?', '2 – SGST', '1 – CGST', '3 – UTGST', '4 – SAC', 'b', 1, 'Match each GST component with the government that levies it.', 'CGST is Central GST and is levied by the Central Government.
Option A: assigning the state levy to the Centre.
Option C: assigning the Union Territory levy to the Centre.
Option D: confusing a service classification code with a tax component.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0360', 9, 'Which GST component is levied by the State Government?', '1 – SGST', '2 – CGST', '3 – UTGST', '4 – IGST', 'a', 1, 'Identify the state-level GST component.', 'SGST is State GST and is levied by the State Government.
Option B: swapping the state and central components.
Option C: confusing the Union Territory component with the state component.
Option D: confusing integrated GST with state GST.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0361', 9, 'Services are classified using which code?', '2 – HSN', '3 – GSTIN', '4 – CGST', '1 – SAC', 'd', 1, 'Use the code specifically assigned to services.', 'SAC is the Service Accounting Code used to classify services.
Option A: using the goods-classification code.
Option B: using a dealer identification number.
Option C: using a tax component instead of a classification code.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0362', 9, 'A dealer''s identification number under GST is called what?', '2 – HSN', '3 – SAC', '1 – GSTIN', '4 – ITC', 'c', 1, 'Distinguish dealer identification from goods and service classification.', 'GSTIN stands for Goods and Service Tax Identification Number and identifies dealers.
Option A: using the goods classification code.
Option B: using the services classification code.
Option D: using a tax-credit concept instead of an identification number.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0363', 9, 'A company divides its whole capital into small equal units. What is each unit called?', '2 – Brokerage', '1 – Share', '3 – Dividend', '4 – Market value', 'b', 1, 'Identify the term for a unit of company capital.', 'Each small unit into which the whole capital is divided is a share.
Option A: confusing the unit of ownership with the broker''s charge.
Option C: confusing ownership units with distributed profit.
Option D: confusing the unit itself with its trading price.'),
        ('mth11b-09-mcq-0364', 9, 'Which type of share generally gives a fixed periodic income but generally does not give voting rights?', '1 – Preference share', '2 – Ordinary share', '3 – Market share', '4 – Treasury share', 'a', 1, 'Recall the distinction between ordinary and preference shares described by the material.', 'Preference shares entitle the holder to a fixed periodic income and generally do not give voting rights.
Option B: assigning the voting-oriented ordinary-share feature to preference shares.
Option C: using a market term that is not a share type described here.
Option D: using a term not described as a share type in the material.')
    ) AS v (question_code, chapter_no, prompt, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, difficulty, hint, explanation)
) AS s
ON t.question_code = s.question_code
WHEN MATCHED AND (t.subject, t.chapter_no, t.prompt, t.option_a, t.option_b, t.option_c, t.option_d, t.correct_option, t.difficulty, t.hint, t.explanation) IS DISTINCT FROM (s.subject, s.chapter_no, s.prompt, s.option_a, s.option_b, s.option_c, s.option_d, s.correct_option, s.difficulty, s.hint, s.explanation) THEN
    UPDATE SET subject = s.subject, chapter_no = s.chapter_no, prompt = s.prompt, option_a = s.option_a, option_b = s.option_b, option_c = s.option_c, option_d = s.option_d, correct_option = s.correct_option, difficulty = s.difficulty, hint = s.hint, explanation = s.explanation
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (question_code, subject, chapter_no, prompt, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, difficulty, hint, explanation) VALUES (s.question_code, s.subject, s.chapter_no, s.prompt, s.option_a, s.option_b, s.option_c, s.option_d, s.correct_option, s.difficulty, s.hint, s.explanation)
WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE AND t.subject = 'maths2' THEN
    DELETE;
